{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N3b7BfTDozyfSJcUWLjNZT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n4\t//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n5\t//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n6\t//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n7\t//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n8\t//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n9\t//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n10\t//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n11\t//! companions.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every function body is\n14\t//! `todo!()`.  Bodies and the empty test module land in later\n15\t//! phases.\n16\t//!\n17\t//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n18\t//!\n19\t//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n20\t//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n21\t//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n22\t//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n23\t//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n24\t//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n25\t//!   writer alive between calls.\n26\t//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` / `file_delete` are real OS file\n27\t//!   primitives.  They are stubbed here as opaque [`File`] handles;\n28\t//!   Phase 3 will wire them to `std::fs::File` behind the\n29\t//!   (not-yet-defined) `std` Cargo feature.\n30\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n31\t//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n32\t//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n33\t//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n34\t//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n35\t//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n36\t//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n37\t//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n38\t//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n39\t//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n40\t//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n41\t//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n42\t//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n43\t//! * Threading primitives ([`Thread`], [`Mutex`], [`Event`]) are\n44\t//!   explicitly out of scope for v1 (\"threading dropped, revisit in\n45\t//!   v2\" per `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).  The types are kept as opaque\n46\t//!   placeholders so signatures can land; the bodies stay `todo!()`\n47\t//!   and a Phase 3 review will decide whether to drop them outright\n48\t//!   or feature-gate.\n49\t//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n50\t//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n51\t//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n52\t//!   are deferred to Phase 3 (where the C call sites get\n53\t//!   translated) — `quic_t` has no `wake_file` /\n54\t//!   `wake_line` fields yet, so the macro can't be expressed in\n55\t//!   Rust without redesigning that type.\n56\t\n57\tuse core::cmp::Ordering;\n58\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n59\t\n60\tuse crate::Error;\n61\tuse crate::{ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n62\t\n63\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n64\t// Tracing / file-id constants.\n65\t//\n66\t// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n67\t// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n68\t// itself is deferred — see the module docstring.\n69\t\n70\t/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\n71\tpub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n72\t/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\n73\tpub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n74\t/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\n75\tpub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n76\t/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\n77\tpub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n78\t/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\n79\tpub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n80\t\n81\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n82\t// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n83\t\n84\t/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n85\t/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\n86\tpub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n87\t    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n88\t}\n89\t\n90\t/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n91\t/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\n92\tpub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n93\t    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n94\t}\n95\t\n96\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n97\t// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n98\t// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n99\t// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n100\t\n101\t/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\n102\tpub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n103\t/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\n104\tpub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n105\t\n106\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n107\t// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n108\t\n109\t/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n110\t/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n111\t/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\n112\tpub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n113\t\n114\t/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n115\t/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n116\t/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n117\t/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n118\t/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n119\t/// alternative.\n120\tpub fn debug_set_stream(_stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n121\t    todo!()\n122\t}\n123\t\n124\t/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n125\t/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n126\t/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n127\t/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n128\t/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\n129\tpub fn debug_printf(_msg: &str) {\n130\t    todo!()\n131\t}\n132\t\n133\t/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n134\t/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n135\t/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n136\t/// to restore.\n137\tpub fn debug_printf_push_stream(_stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n138\t    todo!()\n139\t}\n140\t\n141\t/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n142\t/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\n143\tpub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n144\t    todo!()\n145\t}\n146\t\n147\t/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n148\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\n149\tpub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n150\t    todo!()\n151\t}\n152\t\n153\t/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n154\t/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\n155\tpub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n156\t    todo!()\n157\t}\n158\t\n159\t/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n160\t/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n161\t/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n162\t/// `bool`.\n163\tpub fn debug_printf_reset(_suspended: bool) -> bool {\n164\t    todo!()\n165\t}\n166\t\n167\t/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n168\t/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n169\t/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n170\t/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\n171\tpub fn debug_dump(_bytes: &[u8]) {\n172\t    todo!()\n173\t}\n174\t\n175\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n176\t// String utilities.\n177\t\n178\t// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n179\t// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n180\t// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n181\t// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n182\t// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n183\t\n184\t/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n185\t/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n186\t/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n187\t/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n188\t///\n189\t/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n190\t/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n191\t/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n192\t/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n193\t/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\n194\tpub fn sprintf(_buf: &mut [u8], _msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n195\t    todo!()\n196\t}\n197\t\n198\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n199\t// Connection-id helpers.\n200\t\n201\t/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n202\t/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\n203\tpub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n204\t    id: [0; 20],\n205\t    id_len: 0,\n206\t};\n207\t\n208\t/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n209\t/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n210\t/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n211\t///\n212\t/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n213\t/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n214\t/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n215\t/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\n216\tpub fn format_connection_id(_bytes: &mut [u8], _cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n217\t    todo!()\n218\t}\n219\t\n220\t/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n221\t/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n222\t/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n223\t/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n224\t///\n225\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n226\t/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n227\t/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n228\t/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\n229\tpub fn parse_connection_id(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n230\t    todo!()\n231\t}\n232\t\n233\t// `is_null` / cmp / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n234\t// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n235\t// C source.  `Ord` for `ConnectionId` derives the lexicographic\n236\t// byte-then-length order the C `compare_connection_id` produced.\n237\t\n238\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n239\t// Address helpers.\n240\t\n241\t/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n242\t/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n243\t/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n244\t/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n245\t/// address + 2 for the port).\n246\tpub fn hash_addr_bytes(_addr: &SocketAddr, _bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n247\t    todo!()\n248\t}\n249\t\n250\t/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n251\t/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n252\t/// hash_seed)`.\n253\tpub fn hash_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr, _hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n254\t    todo!()\n255\t}\n256\t\n257\t/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n258\t/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n259\t/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n260\t/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n261\t///\n262\t/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\n263\tpub fn parse_hexa(_hex_input: &str, _bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n264\t    todo!()\n265\t}\n266\t\n267\t/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n268\t/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n269\t/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n270\t/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n271\t/// `Result` return.\n272\tpub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(_hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n273\t    todo!()\n274\t}\n275\t\n276\t/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n277\t/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n278\t/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n279\t///\n280\t/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n281\t/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n282\t/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\n283\tpub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n284\t    _w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n285\t    _connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n286\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n287\t    todo!()\n288\t}\n289\t\n290\t/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n291\t/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n292\t/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\n293\tpub fn compare_addr(_expected: &SocketAddr, _actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n294\t    todo!()\n295\t}\n296\t\n297\t/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n298\t/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n299\t/// struct sockaddr*)`.\n300\tpub fn compare_ip_addr(_expected: &SocketAddr, _actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n301\t    todo!()\n302\t}\n303\t\n304\t/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n305\t/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n306\tpub fn get_addr_port(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n307\t    todo!()\n308\t}\n309\t\n310\t/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n311\t/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n312\t/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n313\t/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\n314\tpub fn set_addr_port(_addr: &mut SocketAddr, _port: u16) {\n315\t    todo!()\n316\t}\n317\t\n318\t/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n319\t/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n320\t/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n321\t/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n322\t/// `usize`.\n323\tpub fn addr_length(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n324\t    todo!()\n325\t}\n326\t\n327\t/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n328\t/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n329\t/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n330\t/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n331\t///\n332\t/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n333\t/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\n334\tpub fn store_addr(_addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n335\t    todo!()\n336\t}\n337\t\n338\t/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n339\t/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n340\t/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n341\t/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n342\t/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\n343\tpub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n344\t    todo!()\n345\t}\n346\t\n347\t/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n348\t/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n349\t/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n350\t/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\n351\tpub fn store_text_addr(_ip_address_text: &str, _port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n352\t    todo!()\n353\t}\n354\t\n355\t/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n356\t/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n357\t/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n358\t/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n359\t/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\n360\tpub fn addr_text(\n361\t    _addr: &SocketAddr,\n362\t    _w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n363\t) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n364\t    todo!()\n365\t}\n366\t\n367\t/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n368\t/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n369\t/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n370\t/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n371\t/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n372\t///\n373\t/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n374\t/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\n375\tpub fn store_loopback_addr(_addr_family: i32, _port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n376\t    todo!()\n377\t}\n378\t\n379\t/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n380\t/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n381\t/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n382\t/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n383\t///\n384\t/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n385\t/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n386\t/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n387\t/// the C behaviour.\n388\tpub fn set_preferred_address(\n389\t    _preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n390\t    _v4_text: Option<&str>,\n391\t    _v6_text: Option<&str>,\n392\t    _preferred_port: u16,\n393\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n394\t    todo!()\n395\t}\n396\t\n397\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n398\t// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n399\t\n400\t/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n401\t/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n402\t/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\n403\tpub fn set_solution_dir(_solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n404\t    todo!()\n405\t}\n406\t\n407\t/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n408\t/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n409\t/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\n410\tpub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n411\t    todo!()\n412\t}\n413\t\n414\t/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n415\t/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n416\t/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n417\t/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n418\t/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\n419\tpub fn get_input_path(\n420\t    _target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n421\t    _solution_path: Option<&str>,\n422\t    _file_name: &str,\n423\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n424\t    todo!()\n425\t}\n426\t\n427\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n428\t// Portable file open / close.\n429\t//\n430\t// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n431\t// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n432\t// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n433\t//\n434\t// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n435\t// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n436\t// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n437\t// feature.\n438\t\n439\t/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n440\t/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\n441\tpub fn file_delete(_file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n442\t    todo!()\n443\t}\n444\t\n445\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n446\t// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n447\t//\n448\t// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n449\t// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n450\t// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n451\t// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n452\t// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n453\t// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n454\t// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n455\t// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n456\t\n457\t/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n458\t/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\n459\tpub fn frames_fixed_skip(_bytes: &[u8], _size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n460\t    todo!()\n461\t}\n462\t\n463\t/// Skip a varint-encoded field; returns the suffix.  C:\n464\t/// `frames_varint_skip`.\n465\tpub fn frames_varint_skip(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n466\t    todo!()\n467\t}\n468\t\n469\t/// Decode a varint and return the suffix plus the value.  C:\n470\t/// `frames_varint_decode` with the `*n64` out-parameter\n471\t/// folded into the tuple return.\n472\tpub fn frames_varint_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n473\t    todo!()\n474\t}\n475\t\n476\t/// Decode a length-prefixed field's length component as a\n477\t/// `usize`.  C: `frames_varlen_decode`.\n478\tpub fn frames_varlen_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], usize)> {\n479\t    todo!()\n480\t}\n481\t\n482\t/// Decode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_decode`.\n483\tpub fn frames_uint8_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u8)> {\n484\t    todo!()\n485\t}\n486\t\n487\t/// Decode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_decode`.\n488\tpub fn frames_uint16_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u16)> {\n489\t    todo!()\n490\t}\n491\t\n492\t/// Decode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_decode`.\n493\tpub fn frames_uint32_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u32)> {\n494\t    todo!()\n495\t}\n496\t\n497\t/// Decode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_decode`.\n498\tpub fn frames_uint64_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], u64)> {\n499\t    todo!()\n500\t}\n501\t\n502\t/// Skip a length-prefixed data field.  C:\n503\t/// `frames_length_data_skip`.\n504\tpub fn frames_length_data_skip(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n505\t    todo!()\n506\t}\n507\t\n508\t/// Decode a connection id and return the suffix.  C:\n509\t/// `frames_cid_decode`.  The output parameter folds into\n510\t/// the tuple return.\n511\tpub fn frames_cid_decode(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Option<(&[u8], ConnectionId)> {\n512\t    todo!()\n513\t}\n514\t\n515\t/// Length of the varint encoding of `n64`.  C:\n516\t/// `size_t frames_varint_encode_length(uint64_t n64)`.\n517\tpub fn frames_varint_encode_length(_n64: u64) -> usize {\n518\t    todo!()\n519\t}\n520\t\n521\t/// Decode the wire length of a varint from its first byte.  C\n522\t/// macro: `VARINT_LEN(bytes) (((uint8_t)1) << ((bytes[0] >> 6) & 3))`.\n523\t/// The macro takes the whole buffer; the function takes just the\n524\t/// first byte (the rest is unused in the C expansion).\n525\tpub const fn varint_len(byte0: u8) -> usize {\n526\t    1usize << ((byte0 >> 6) & 3)\n527\t}\n528\t\n529\t/// Encode a varint into `bytes`; returns the unused tail or `None`\n530\t/// if the buffer was too small.  C: `frames_varint_encode`.\n531\tpub fn frames_varint_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n64: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n532\t    todo!()\n533\t}\n534\t\n535\t/// Encode a `usize` as a varint length prefix.  C:\n536\t/// `frames_varlen_encode`.\n537\tpub fn frames_varlen_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: usize) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n538\t    todo!()\n539\t}\n540\t\n541\t/// Encode a single byte.  C: `frames_uint8_encode`.\n542\tpub fn frames_uint8_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: u8) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n543\t    todo!()\n544\t}\n545\t\n546\t/// Encode a network-order `u16`.  C: `frames_uint16_encode`.\n547\tpub fn frames_uint16_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: u16) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n548\t    todo!()\n549\t}\n550\t\n551\t/// Encode a 24-bit value (low three bytes of `n`) in network\n552\t/// order.  C: `frames_uint24_encode`.\n553\tpub fn frames_uint24_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n554\t    todo!()\n555\t}\n556\t\n557\t/// Encode a network-order `u32`.  C: `frames_uint32_encode`.\n558\tpub fn frames_uint32_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: u32) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n559\t    todo!()\n560\t}\n561\t\n562\t/// Encode a network-order `u64`.  C: `frames_uint64_encode`.\n563\tpub fn frames_uint64_encode(_bytes: &mut [u8], _n: u64) -> Option<&mut [u8]> {\n564\t    todo!()\n565\t}\n566\t\n567\t/// Encode a length prefix followed by the data bytes.  C:\n568\t/// `frames_length_data_encode`.  The C signature took\n569\t/// `(size_t l, const uint8_t* v)` — collapsed to `&[u8]` (slice\n570\t/// length subsumes `l`).\n571\tpub fn frames_length_data_encode<'a>(_bytes: &'a mut [u8], _v: &[u8]) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n572\t    todo!()\n573\t}\n574\t\n575\t/// Encode a connection id as length-prefixed data.  C:\n576\t/// `frames_cid_encode`.\n577\tpub fn frames_cid_encode<'a>(_bytes: &'a mut [u8], _cid: &ConnectionId) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n578\t    todo!()\n579\t}\n580\t\n581\t/// Encode a NUL-terminated C string `s` as length-prefixed data\n582\t/// (the NUL is *not* written).  C: `frames_charz_encode`.\n583\tpub fn frames_charz_encode<'a>(_bytes: &'a mut [u8], _s: &str) -> Option<&'a mut [u8]> {\n584\t    todo!()\n585\t}\n586\t\n587\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n588\t// Constant-time memcmp (used for reset secrets).\n589\t\n590\t/// Constant-time three-way compare of two byte buffers of equal\n591\t/// length.  C: `int constant_time_memcmp(const uint8_t*\n592\t/// x, const uint8_t* y, size_t l)` — returning negative / zero /\n593\t/// positive, mapped to [`Ordering`].  The two slice lengths are\n594\t/// the C `l` argument and must match.\n595\tpub fn constant_time_memcmp(_x: &[u8], _y: &[u8]) -> Ordering {\n596\t    todo!()\n597\t}\n598\t\n599\t// The C `picoquic_thread_t` / `picoquic_mutex_t` / `picoquic_event_t`\n600\t// portability wrappers around pthread / Win32 are gone.  Rust call\n601\t// sites use the standard library directly: `std::thread::JoinHandle`\n602\t// for a thread handle, `std::sync::Mutex<T>` for a lock paired with\n603\t// the value it guards, and `(Mutex<bool>, Condvar)` (or a higher-level\n604\t// channel from `std::sync::mpsc`) where C used `picoquic_event_t`.\n605\t//\n606\t// Likewise the `picoquic_thread_fn` typedef has no Rust counterpart —\n607\t// `std::thread::spawn` already takes an arbitrary `FnOnce() + Send`\n608\t// closure, which subsumes the C \"function pointer + void* arg\"\n609\t// pattern.\n610\t\n611\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n612\t// Random-number helpers.\n613\t\n614\t// `uniform_random` is gone -- the C body read `/dev/urandom`\n615\t// at every call.  Rust callers use `rand::Rng::random_range`\n616\t// on whatever RNG they hold (`Quic.rng` for the secure stream,\n617\t// `rand::rng()` for the non-secure thread-local).\n618\t\n619\t// `test_random` and the `test_*_random` family moved to\n620\t// `crate::tests::util` — they only support the test suite.\n621\t\n622\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n623\t// Byte-array → text conversion (logging helper).\n624\t\n625\t/// Translate a byte buffer to a printable text form.  C:\n626\t/// `char* uint8_to_str(char* text, size_t text_len, const\n627\t/// uint8_t* data, size_t data_len)` — both length parameters fold\n628\t/// into the slice lengths.  The returned slice is the populated\n629\t/// prefix of `text`.\n630\tpub fn uint8_to_str<'a>(_text: &'a mut [u8], _data: &[u8]) -> &'a [u8] {\n631\t    todo!()\n632\t}\n633\t\n634\t// `TestSimPacket`, `TestAqm`, `JitterMode`, and `TestSimLink`\n635\t// moved to `crate::tests::util` — they translate the\n636\t// `picoquictest/sim_link.c` test-only network simulator.\n637\t// `TEST_SNI` and the `TEST_FILE_*` / `TEST_ECH_*` cert-path\n638\t// constants likewise moved over.\n639\t\n640\t#[cfg(test)]\n641\tmod test {}\n642\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"2b4138a7-d548-42b0-bb10-d53792210a67","timestamp":"2026-05-05T17:01:44.358Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/utils.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/utils.h`.\n//!\n//! Grab-bag of helpers used throughout the C library: debug\n//! tracing, sprintf-style formatting, connection-id and address\n//! manipulation, file open/close wrappers, varint and fixed-width\n//! frame field codecs, constant-time memcmp, threading primitives,\n//! a deterministic test RNG, and the test-suite's network simulator\n//! (sim_link).  No matching `.c` file lives next to the header —\n//! the bodies are in `quic/util.c` (most of the API), with a\n//! handful of helpers in `quic/utils.c`-style\n//! companions.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every function body is\n//! `todo!()`.  Bodies and the empty test module land in later\n//! phases.\n//!\n//! Translation policy notes for this module:\n//!\n//! * `FILE *` → `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` for *write* sinks\n//!   (mirrors the convention introduced in\n//!   [`crate::logger`] and\n//!   [`crate::config`]).  The persistent\n//!   debug-output stream installed by [`debug_set_stream`]\n//!   transfers ownership instead, so the global slot can keep the\n//!   writer alive between calls.\n//! * `file_open*` / `file_close` / `file_delete` are real OS file\n//!   primitives.  They are stubbed here as opaque [`File`] handles;\n//!   Phase 3 will wire them to `std::fs::File` behind the\n//!   (not-yet-defined) `std` Cargo feature.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` parameters and fields use\n//!   [`core::net::SocketAddr`] — same convention as\n//!   [`crate`].  `struct sockaddr_storage*`\n//!   output parameters fold into `Option<SocketAddr>` (the C\n//!   `AF_UNSPEC` sentinel becomes `None`).\n//! * `int`-valued comparators (`compare_addr`,\n//!   `compare_ip_addr`, `compare_connection_id`,\n//!   `constant_time_memcmp`) return [`core::cmp::Ordering`]\n//!   when they are real three-way comparators; the `Boolean-ish`\n//!   ones (`is_connection_id_null`) become `bool`.\n//! * `char*` strings: input strings borrow as `&str`; owned outputs\n//!   return `String`.  Buffer-supplying helpers (e.g.\n//!   [`addr_text`]) take a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write`.\n//! * Threading primitives ([`Thread`], [`Mutex`], [`Event`]) are\n//!   explicitly out of scope for v1 (\"threading dropped, revisit in\n//!   v2\" per `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).  The types are kept as opaque\n//!   placeholders so signatures can land; the bodies stay `todo!()`\n//!   and a Phase 3 review will decide whether to drop them outright\n//!   or feature-gate.\n//! * The C macro `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` and the\n//!   `DBG_PRINTF` family are bodies-not-signatures: they expand at\n//!   the call site rather than being declared in the header.  They\n//!   are deferred to Phase 3 (where the C call sites get\n//!   translated) — `quic_t` has no `wake_file` /\n//!   `wake_line` fields yet, so the macro can't be expressed in\n//!   Rust without redesigning that type.\n\nuse core::cmp::Ordering;\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::{ConnectionId, PreferredAddress};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Tracing / file-id constants.\n//\n// Used by the C `SET_LAST_WAKE(quic, file_id)` macro to record\n// which translation unit last poked the QUIC context.  The macro\n// itself is deferred — see the module docstring.\n\n/// File-id constant for `sender.c`.\npub const SENDER: u32 = 1;\n/// File-id constant for `packet.c`.\npub const PACKET: u32 = 2;\n/// File-id constant for `quicctx.c`.\npub const QUICCTX: u32 = 3;\n/// File-id constant for `frames.c`.\npub const FRAME: u32 = 4;\n/// File-id constant for `loss_recovery.c`.\npub const LOSS_RECOVERY: u32 = 5;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Rate / time / byte conversion (function-like macros in C).\n\n/// Bytes that fit on a link of `bps` bytes per second over\n/// `microseconds`.  C: `BYTES_FROM_RATE`.\npub const fn bytes_from_rate(microseconds: u64, bps: u64) -> u64 {\n    microseconds.wrapping_mul(bps) / 1_000_000\n}\n\n/// Effective rate (bytes per second) implied by sending `bytes`\n/// in `microseconds`.  C: `RATE_FROM_BYTES`.\npub const fn rate_from_bytes(bytes: u64, microseconds: u64) -> u64 {\n    bytes.wrapping_mul(1_000_000) / microseconds\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Filesystem path constants — Linux/macOS layout (the only target\n// supported in v1; `_WINDOWS` paths are dropped per\n// `TRANSLATE_PLAN.md`).\n\n/// Default solution-relative path used by the test fixtures.\npub const DEFAULT_SOLUTION_DIR: &str = \"./\";\n/// Path separator used to assemble fixture paths.\npub const FILE_SEPARATOR: &str = \"/\";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Debug-printf knobs and tracing helpers.\n\n/// Maximum number of trailing characters of `__FILE__` shown by\n/// the C `DBG_PRINTF` macro (`__FILE__` is right-truncated to the\n/// last `DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX` bytes).\npub const DBG_PRINTF_FILENAME_MAX: usize = 24;\n\n/// Install (or clear, with `None`) the global debug-output sink.\n/// The C signature `void debug_set_stream(FILE *F)` takes a\n/// borrowed `FILE*`; Rust keeps the sink alive across calls by\n/// transferring ownership of a `Box<dyn Write>`.  Phase 3 will\n/// decide whether to expose a borrowed-with-`'static`-lifetime\n/// alternative.\npub fn debug_set_stream(_stream: Option<Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>>) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// `printf`-style write to the installed debug sink.  The C\n/// variadic signature `void debug_printf(const char* fmt, ...)`\n/// becomes a sink for already-formatted text — Rust call sites\n/// will use `format_args!` / `write!` directly.  The string is\n/// borrowed for the duration of the call.\npub fn debug_printf(_msg: &str) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Push the current debug sink and install a new one.  Mirrors\n/// `void debug_printf_push_stream(FILE* f)`.  The previously\n/// installed sink (if any) is saved for [`debug_printf_pop_stream`]\n/// to restore.\npub fn debug_printf_push_stream(_stream: Box<dyn core::fmt::Write>) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Pop the most recently pushed debug sink and restore the saved\n/// one.  Mirrors `void debug_printf_pop_stream(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_pop_stream() {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Stop logging through the debug sink without tearing it down.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_suspend(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_suspend() {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Resume logging through the previously installed debug sink.\n/// Mirrors `void debug_printf_resume(void)`.\npub fn debug_printf_resume() {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Force the debug suspension flag to `suspended` and return the\n/// previous value.  C: `int debug_printf_reset(int suspended)`.\n/// The flag is a 0/1 indicator on both sides, so it maps to\n/// `bool`.\npub fn debug_printf_reset(_suspended: bool) -> bool {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Hex-dump `bytes` to the debug sink.  C: `void debug_dump(const\n/// void *x, int len)` — only declared when `_DEBUG` is defined,\n/// so this entry point is informational in v1.  The C `int len`\n/// is non-negative in every observed call; mapped to slice length.\npub fn debug_dump(_bytes: &[u8]) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// String utilities.\n\n// `picoquic_string_create`, `picoquic_string_duplicate`, and\n// `picoquic_string_free` are subsumed by Rust's owned `String`:\n// `String::from(s)` replaces the duplicate / create pair, and the\n// `Drop` impl replaces the `free` shim.  Phase 3 callers should use\n// owned `String` directly rather than going through helpers.\n\n/// Format `args` into the head of `buf` and report bytes written\n/// via `nb_chars`; truncation returns an `Err`.  C:\n/// `int sprintf(char* buf, size_t buf_len, size_t* nb_chars,\n/// const char* fmt, ...)`.\n///\n/// The C variadic signature collapses to a single\n/// pre-formatted `&str` argument, mirroring the convention used\n/// by [`debug_printf`].  Buffer-overflow returns `Err(())`\n/// (matching the C `-1`); on success the `Ok(usize)` is the byte\n/// count written, replacing the `nb_chars` out-parameter.\npub fn sprintf(_buf: &mut [u8], _msg: &str) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Connection-id helpers.\n\n/// All-zero connection id used as a sentinel for \"unset\" / \"none\".\n/// C: `extern const ConnectionId null_connection_id`.\npub const NULL_CONNECTION_ID: ConnectionId = ConnectionId {\n    id: [0; 20],\n    id_len: 0,\n};\n\n/// Format the connection id into `bytes` and return the number of\n/// bytes written.  C: `uint8_t format_connection_id(uint8_t* bytes,\n/// size_t bytes_max, ConnectionId connection_id)`.\n///\n/// Pointer-shape choice: the C body writes through `bytes` for\n/// `bytes_max` bytes and reports the populated prefix length, so\n/// `&mut [u8]` carries both pieces of information.  The connection\n/// id is `Copy`, kept by value as in C.\npub fn format_connection_id(_bytes: &mut [u8], _cnx_id: ConnectionId) -> u8 {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Parse a connection id of `len` bytes from `bytes` into `connection_id`,\n/// returning the number of bytes consumed.  C:\n/// `uint8_t parse_connection_id(const uint8_t* bytes, uint8_t len,\n/// ConnectionId* connection_id)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Result<ConnectionId, Error>` reports the parsed id on\n/// success, `Err(())` on truncation.  The `len` parameter and the\n/// slice length are redundant in safe Rust; the slice carries it.\npub fn parse_connection_id(_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// `is_null` / cmp / `hash_with_seed` / `val64` live as methods on\n// [`ConnectionId`] (see `crate::lib`); they were free fns in the\n// C source.  `Ord` for `ConnectionId` derives the lexicographic\n// byte-then-length order the C `compare_connection_id` produced.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Address helpers.\n\n/// Pack an IP-and-port into a stable, byte-form key suitable for\n/// hashing.  C: `size_t hash_addr_bytes(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr, uint8_t* bytes)`.  Returns the populated prefix\n/// length; the C body writes at most 18 bytes (16 for the v6\n/// address + 2 for the port).\npub fn hash_addr_bytes(_addr: &SocketAddr, _bytes: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Hash an address with a 16-byte seed.  C: `uint64_t\n/// hash_addr(const struct sockaddr* addr, const uint8_t*\n/// hash_seed)`.\npub fn hash_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr, _hash_seed: &[u8; 16]) -> u64 {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Decode a hex-coded byte string and write the binary form into\n/// `bin_output`.  C: `size_t parse_hexa(char const*\n/// hex_input, size_t input_length, uint8_t* bin_output, size_t\n/// output_max)`.  Returns the number of bytes decoded.\n///\n/// Both length parameters fold into the slice lengths.\npub fn parse_hexa(_hex_input: &str, _bin_output: &mut [u8]) -> usize {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Parse a hex-coded connection id.  C: `uint8_t\n/// parse_connection_id_hexa(char const* hex_input, size_t\n/// input_length, ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning the\n/// number of bytes decoded; the output parameter folds into the\n/// `Result` return.\npub fn parse_connection_id_hexa(_hex_input: &str) -> Result<ConnectionId, Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Print a connection id to a hex string buffer.  C:\n/// `int print_connection_id_hexa(char* buf, size_t buf_len,\n/// const ConnectionId* connection_id)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// The output buffer becomes a `&mut dyn core::fmt::Write` sink to\n/// keep the helper `no_std`-friendly (same convention as the\n/// logger module).  The 0 / -1 status maps to `Result<(), Error>`.\npub fn print_connection_id_hexa(\n    _w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    _connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare two addresses (IP + port).  C:\n/// `int compare_addr(const struct sockaddr* expected,\n/// const struct sockaddr* actual)`.\npub fn compare_addr(_expected: &SocketAddr, _actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Three-way compare just the IP component of two addresses.  C:\n/// `int compare_ip_addr(const struct sockaddr*, const\n/// struct sockaddr*)`.\npub fn compare_ip_addr(_expected: &SocketAddr, _actual: &SocketAddr) -> Ordering {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Read the port number out of an address.  C: `uint16_t\n/// get_addr_port(const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\npub fn get_addr_port(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> u16 {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Replace the port number on an address.  C declares this with\n/// `const struct sockaddr*` but the body casts away const and\n/// writes through the pointer (`util.c:531`).  The Rust signature\n/// takes a `&mut SocketAddr` to reflect the actual contract.\npub fn set_addr_port(_addr: &mut SocketAddr, _port: u16) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Length of the platform sockaddr representation in bytes —\n/// `sizeof(sockaddr_in)` for v4, `sizeof(sockaddr_in6)` for v6,\n/// `0` for `AF_UNSPEC`.  C: `int addr_length(const struct\n/// sockaddr* addr)`.  The return value is non-negative; mapped to\n/// `usize`.\npub fn addr_length(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> usize {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Copy an address into a sockaddr_storage slot, treating\n/// `addr == None` as the C \"zero out the storage\" path.  C:\n/// `void store_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const struct sockaddr* addr)`.\n///\n/// The C output parameter becomes the function return:\n/// `Option<SocketAddr>` represents stored / cleared respectively.\npub fn store_addr(_addr: Option<&SocketAddr>) -> Option<SocketAddr> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Return the IP-bytes (4 or 16) inside a sockaddr.  C:\n/// `void get_ip_addr(struct sockaddr* addr, uint8_t**\n/// ip_addr, uint8_t* ip_addr_len)` — both output parameters fold\n/// into the returned slice.  `None` matches the C path where\n/// `*ip_addr = NULL; *ip_addr_len = 0;` for unsupported families.\npub fn get_ip_addr(_addr: &SocketAddr) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Parse `ip_address_text` (IPv4 or IPv6 textual form) and combine\n/// with `port` into a [`SocketAddr`].  C:\n/// `int store_text_addr(struct sockaddr_storage* stored_addr,\n/// const char* ip_address_text, uint16_t port)` returning 0 / -1.\npub fn store_text_addr(_ip_address_text: &str, _port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Print an address (IP + port) to a `core::fmt::Write` sink.  C:\n/// `char const* addr_text(const struct sockaddr* addr,\n/// char* text, size_t text_size)` returns the populated prefix of\n/// `text`; the Rust translation writes through a sink to stay\n/// `no_std`-friendly.  The `Err` arm propagates a fmt error.\npub fn addr_text(\n    _addr: &SocketAddr,\n    _w: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n) -> Result<(), core::fmt::Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Build the loopback address for the given family + port.  C:\n/// `int store_loopback_addr(struct sockaddr_storage*\n/// stored_addr, int addr_family, uint16_t port)` — the family\n/// argument is one of `AF_INET` / `AF_INET6` and folds into the\n/// `SocketAddr` variant.\n///\n/// `addr_family` stays an `i32` to keep parity with the C ABI;\n/// callers in the codebase pass the `AF_*` constants directly.\npub fn store_loopback_addr(_addr_family: i32, _port: u16) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Fill a preferred-address transport parameter from textual IPv4\n/// and/or IPv6 addresses.  C: `int set_preferred_address(\n/// TpPreferredAddress* preferred, char const* v4_text,\n/// char const* v6_text, uint16_t preferred_port)` returning 0 / -1.\n///\n/// Either text argument may be `None` (the C `NULL` selector for\n/// \"skip this family\").  The function mutates `preferred` in\n/// place; on `Err(())` the partial state is unspecified, matching\n/// the C behaviour.\npub fn set_preferred_address(\n    _preferred: &mut PreferredAddress,\n    _v4_text: Option<&str>,\n    _v6_text: Option<&str>,\n    _preferred_port: u16,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Solution-dir helpers (test fixtures).\n\n/// Set the global solution-relative root used by the test\n/// fixtures.  C: `void set_solution_dir(char const*\n/// solution_dir)`.  Passing `None` clears the override.\npub fn set_solution_dir(_solution_dir: Option<&str>) {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Read the global solution-relative root.  C exposes a raw\n/// `extern char const* solution_dir;` — translated as a\n/// getter so the global stays behind a safe interface.\npub fn solution_dir() -> Option<&'static str> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Compose `solution_path/file_name` (or `./file_name` when\n/// `solution_path` is `None`) into `target_file_path`.  C:\n/// `int get_input_path(char* target_file_path, size_t\n/// file_path_max, const char* solution_path, const char*\n/// file_name)`.  Buffer overflow returns `Err(())`.\npub fn get_input_path(\n    _target_file_path: &mut dyn core::fmt::Write,\n    _solution_path: Option<&str>,\n    _file_name: &str,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Portable file open / close.\n//\n// `picoquic_file_open*` / `picoquic_file_close` only existed to\n// paper over Windows's `fopen_s` quirk; the Rust translation uses\n// `std::fs::File` directly at every call site (close = Drop).\n//\n// `picoquic_file_delete` survives because callers want a stable\n// no_std-friendly façade for \"remove this file by name\".  Phase 4\n// routes the body through `std::fs::remove_file` under the `std`\n// feature.\n\n/// Delete a file by name, returning the OS errno on failure.  C:\n/// `int picoquic_file_delete(char const* file_name, int* last_err)`.\npub fn file_delete(_file_name: &(impl AsRef<std::path::Path> + ?Sized)) -> Result<(), i32> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Frame skip / decode / encode helpers.\n//\n// The C entry points all share a \"cursor pair\" idiom: take\n// `(bytes, bytes_max)`, advance the cursor and return a pointer\n// past the parsed field, or `NULL` on overrun.  In Rust the\n// `&[u8]` slice carries both endpoints; the return value is the\n// remaining suffix on success or `None` on overrun (the natural\n// shape of the Rust slice idiom).  Decoded values fold into the\n// `Ok` payload; encoded outputs fill a pre-allocated buffer and\n// return the unused tail (or `None` if the buffer was too small).\n\n/// Skip `size` bytes from `bytes`; returns the suffix or `None`\n/// if `bytes` is too short.  C: `frames_fixed_skip`.\npub fn frames_fixed_skip(_bytes: &[u8], _size: u64) -> Option<&[u8]> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Skip a varint-encoded field; retur