{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GLq6FFSFca19ASr4W8SYtW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n4\t//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n5\t//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n6\t//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n7\t//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n8\t//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n9\t//!\n10\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n11\t//! Phase 4 fills in the bodies.\n12\t//!\n13\t//! ## Shape conventions\n14\t//!\n15\t//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n16\t//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n17\t//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n18\t//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n19\t//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n20\t//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n21\t//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n22\t//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n23\t//!   parameters remain in this module.\n24\t//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n25\t//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n26\t//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n27\t//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n28\t//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n29\t//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n30\t//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n31\t//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n32\t//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n33\t//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n34\t//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n35\t//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n36\t//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n37\t//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n38\t//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n39\t//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n40\t//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n41\t//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n42\t//!   already holds storage.\n43\t//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n44\t//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n45\t//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n46\t//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n47\t//!   reused here.\n48\t//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n49\t//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n50\t//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n51\t//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n52\t//!\n53\t//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n54\t//!\n55\t//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n56\t//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n57\t//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n58\t//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n59\t//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n60\t//!\n61\t//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n62\t//!\n63\t//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n64\t//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n65\t//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n66\t//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n67\t//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n68\t//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n69\t//! pairs can coexist.\n70\t//!\n71\t//! ## Out of scope\n72\t//!\n73\t//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n74\t//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n75\t//!   source and are not translated.\n76\t//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n77\t//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n78\t//!   not translated.\n79\t\n80\textern crate alloc;\n81\t\n82\tuse alloc::boxed::Box;\n83\tuse alloc::vec::Vec;\n84\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n85\t\n86\tuse crate::Instant;\n87\tuse crate::internal::CryptoContext;\n88\tuse crate::{Connection, ConnectionId, Error, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE};\n89\t\n90\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n91\t// Label constants (C `#define` → `&str`).\n92\t//\n93\t// All labels are NUL-terminated bytes in the C header because tls\n94\t// takes them as `char const*`.  The Rust shape is plain `&str`; the\n95\t// trailing NUL is reintroduced (if needed) at the tls FFI\n96\t// boundary.\n97\t\n98\t/// Initial secret label for the client direction.  C:\n99\t/// `LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT`.\n100\tpub const LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT: &str = \"client in\";\n101\t\n102\t/// Initial secret label for the server direction.  C:\n103\t/// `LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER`.\n104\tpub const LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER: &str = \"server in\";\n105\t\n106\t/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v1.  C:\n107\t/// `LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\n108\tpub const LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quic ku\";\n109\t\n110\t/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v2.  C:\n111\t/// `LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\n112\tpub const LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quicv2 ku\";\n113\t\n114\t/// HKDF \"key\" component label.  C: `LABEL_KEY`.\n115\tpub const LABEL_KEY: &str = \"key\";\n116\t\n117\t/// HKDF \"iv\" component label.  C: `LABEL_IV`.\n118\tpub const LABEL_IV: &str = \"iv\";\n119\t\n120\t/// HKDF \"hp\" (header protection) component label.  C:\n121\t/// `LABEL_HP`.\n122\tpub const LABEL_HP: &str = \"hp\";\n123\t\n124\t/// HKDF \"cid\" component label.  C: `LABEL_CID`.\n125\tpub const LABEL_CID: &str = \"cid\";\n126\t\n127\t/// HKDF label for the global CID encryption secret.  C:\n128\t/// `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL`.\n129\tpub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL: &str = \"cid global\";\n130\t\n131\t/// Number of HKDF rounds run when deriving the global CID secret.\n132\t/// C: `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS`.\n133\tpub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS: u32 = 4;\n134\t\n135\t/// QUIC v1 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n136\t/// `LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE`.\n137\tpub const LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quic \";\n138\t\n139\t/// QUIC v2 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n140\t/// `LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE`.\n141\tpub const LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quicv2 \";\n142\t\n143\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n144\t// Master TLS context.\n145\t//\n146\t// In Phase 2 the C `*mut c_void tls_master_ctx` is replaced by\n147\t// `Quic.tls_client_config` / `tls_server_config` (boxed\n148\t// `DynClientConfig` / `DynServerConfig` trait objects defined in\n149\t// [`crate::tls`]).  Both lifecycle entry points install or\n150\t// release state on the QUIC context itself.\n151\t\n152\timpl Quic {\n153\t    /// Initialize this context's master TLS context.  Loads the\n154\t    /// certificate chain, the private key, the trusted-root bundle\n155\t    /// and the ticket-encryption key.  Any of the file-name / key\n156\t    /// parameters may be `NULL` in C (server-only, no roots, no\n157\t    /// ticket key); Rust models that with `Option<&str>` /\n158\t    /// `Option<&[u8]>`.  C: `master_tlscontext`.\n159\t    pub fn init_master_tls_context(\n160\t        &mut self,\n161\t        _cert_file_name: Option<&str>,\n162\t        _key_file_name: Option<&str>,\n163\t        _cert_root_file_name: Option<&str>,\n164\t        _ticket_key: Option<&[u8]>,\n165\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n166\t        todo!()\n167\t    }\n168\t\n169\t    /// Tear down the master TLS context installed by\n170\t    /// [`Quic::init_master_tls_context`].  C:\n171\t    /// `master_tlscontext_free`.\n172\t    pub fn free_master_tls_context(&mut self) {\n173\t        todo!()\n174\t    }\n175\t}\n176\t\n177\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n178\t// Per-connection TLS context.\n179\t//\n180\t// `tls_ctx_t` itself lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`; this module\n181\t// just exposes the API surface that quic-core uses to drive it.\n182\t\n183\timpl Connection {\n184\t    /// Allocate a per-connection TLS context, attach it to this\n185\t    /// connection, and initialize the tls handshake-property slots.\n186\t    /// C: `tlscontext_create`.  C side returns 0 on success,\n187\t    /// `ERROR_TLS_SERVER_CON_WITHOUT_CERT` / `ERROR_MEMORY` / -1 on\n188\t    /// failure.\n189\t    pub fn create_tls_context(&mut self, _quic: &mut Quic) -> Result<(), Error> {\n190\t        todo!()\n191\t    }\n192\t\n193\t    /// Drop transient buffers (ALPN list, transport-parameter encode\n194\t    /// scratch) once the handshake is done.  C:\n195\t    /// `tlscontext_trim_after_handshake`.\n196\t    pub fn trim_tls_context_after_handshake(&mut self) {\n197\t        todo!()\n198\t    }\n199\t\n200\t    /// Forget the session ticket installed for a 0-RTT attempt.  C:\n201\t    /// `tlscontext_remove_ticket`.\n202\t    pub fn remove_tls_ticket(&mut self) {\n203\t        todo!()\n204\t    }\n205\t}\n206\t\n207\t// `tls_context_free` is gone -- `Connection.tls_ctx` is now an\n208\t// `Option<Box<dyn Session>>`, and `Drop` on the boxed Session\n209\t// runs the backend's teardown automatically.  The C\n210\t// `client_mode` flag is unnecessary too: the `Session` impl\n211\t// knows whether it's a client or server session internally.\n212\t\n213\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n214\t// TLS stream processing.\n215\t\n216\timpl Connection {\n217\t    /// Drive the TLS handshake by feeding in any data buffered on\n218\t    /// the crypto streams and pushing produced data back out.\n219\t    /// Returns the number of bytes consumed (the C `int*\n220\t    /// data_consumed` out-parameter; bytes consumed is non-negative,\n221\t    /// so the type widens to `usize` rather than tracking the C\n222\t    /// `int`).  C: `tls_stream_process`.\n223\t    pub fn process_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n224\t        todo!()\n225\t    }\n226\t\n227\t    /// Report whether the TLS handshake has completed.  C signature\n228\t    /// returned `int` (0/1); promoted to `bool`.  C:\n229\t    /// `is_tls_complete`.\n230\t    pub fn is_tls_complete(&self) -> bool {\n231\t        todo!()\n232\t    }\n233\t\n234\t    /// Send the initial `ClientHello` (or the response to a\n235\t    /// `HelloRetry`) on the TLS stream.  C:\n236\t    /// `initialize_tls_stream`.\n237\t    pub fn initialize_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<(), Error> {\n238\t        todo!()\n239\t    }\n240\t}\n241\t\n242\timpl Quic {\n243\t    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n244\t    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n245\t    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n246\t        todo!()\n247\t    }\n248\t}\n249\t\n250\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n251\t// Random number generation.\n252\t//\n253\t// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n254\t// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n255\t// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n256\t// to `&mut [u8]`.\n257\t\n258\t// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n259\t// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n260\t// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n261\t// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n262\t// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n263\t// non-secure stream.\n264\t\n265\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n266\t// AEAD primitives.\n267\t//\n268\t// The C `aead_*` family wrapped opaque `void*` handles around\n269\t// `ptls_aead_context_t*`.  Phase 2 replaced them with the\n270\t// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] trait — backends supply implementations,\n271\t// the rest of the crate is generic over them.  This module\n272\t// retains only the constant `QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN` (the fixed\n273\t// 16-byte tag QUIC mandates).\n274\t\n275\t/// Length of the QUIC AEAD authentication tag.  All TLS 1.3\n276\t/// cipher suites used by QUIC (RFC 9001 §5.3) produce a 16-byte\n277\t/// tag, so the value is fixed.  Replaces the C\n278\t/// `aead_get_checksum_length(void*)` accessor; once a\n279\t/// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] exists, prefer `key.tag_len()`.\n280\tpub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n281\t\n282\t// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n283\t// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n284\t// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n285\t// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n286\t// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n287\t// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n288\t//\n289\t// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n290\t// trait object replaces them.\n291\t//\n292\t// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n293\t// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n294\t// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n295\t\n296\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n297\t// Initial-secret derivation.\n298\t//\n299\t// RFC 9001 fixes the initial-secret HKDF to SHA-256 (the salt is the\n300\t// version-specific 20-byte constant).  Both helpers therefore commit\n301\t// to SHA-256 internally — no cipher-suite parameter needed (the C\n302\t// API took `&PtlsCipherSuite` purely for parity with later epoch\n303\t// derivations).  `master_secret` / `client_secret` / `server_secret`\n304\t// must be 32 bytes (`Sha256::output_size()`); a wider buffer is\n305\t// fine (caller-provided slots stay flexible).\n306\t\n307\t/// Derive the per-connection-ID initial master secret.  C:\n308\t/// `setup_initial_master_secret`.\n309\tpub fn setup_initial_master_secret(\n310\t    _salt: &[u8],\n311\t    _initial_connection_id: ConnectionId,\n312\t    _master_secret: &mut [u8],\n313\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n314\t    todo!()\n315\t}\n316\t\n317\t/// Derive client/server initial secrets from the master secret.\n318\t/// `client_secret` and `server_secret` are filled in place.  C:\n319\t/// `setup_initial_secrets`.\n320\tpub fn setup_initial_secrets(\n321\t    _master_secret: &[u8],\n322\t    _client_secret: &mut [u8],\n323\t    _server_secret: &mut [u8],\n324\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n325\t    todo!()\n326\t}\n327\t\n328\timpl Connection {\n329\t    /// Set up this connection's per-epoch initial AEAD / PN\n330\t    /// encryption contexts from the connection's initial CID.  C:\n331\t    /// `setup_initial_traffic_keys`.\n332\t    pub fn setup_initial_traffic_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n333\t        todo!()\n334\t    }\n335\t}\n336\t\n337\t/// Output bundle from [`Quic::initial_aead_context`].  C returned\n338\t/// the AEAD and PN contexts through `void**` out-pointers; the Rust\n339\t/// shape collapses both into a struct so the function signature is\n340\t/// one-out, one-return.\n341\tpub struct InitialAeadContext {\n342\t    /// Owned AEAD context for packet protection.\n343\t    pub aead_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>,\n344\t    /// Owned header-protection context.\n345\t    pub pn_enc_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>,\n346\t}\n347\t\n348\timpl Quic {\n349\t    /// Derive an AEAD + PN context for the initial encryption\n350\t    /// level.  `version_index` selects the QUIC version's salt and\n351\t    /// prefix label; `is_client` and `is_enc` pick the\n352\t    /// client/server and encrypt/decrypt directions.  C:\n353\t    /// `get_initial_aead_context`.\n354\t    pub fn initial_aead_context(\n355\t        &mut self,\n356\t        _version_index: i32,\n357\t        _initial_connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n358\t        _is_client: bool,\n359\t        _is_enc: bool,\n360\t    ) -> Result<InitialAeadContext, Error> {\n361\t        todo!()\n362\t    }\n363\t}\n364\t\n365\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n366\t// Application-secret access and rotation.\n367\t//\n368\t// `get_app_secret` returns one of the two\n369\t// `app_secret_*` buffers stashed inside `tls_ctx_t`.  The\n370\t// C signature returned a `uint8_t*` into that buffer; in Rust the\n371\t// natural shape is a borrowed slice carrying the digest length\n372\t// (callers consult `get_app_secret_size` for the length\n373\t// today, which is redundant once the borrow encodes it).  Phase 3\n374\t// may collapse the size accessor.\n375\t\n376\timpl Connection {\n377\t    /// Return a borrow of the app-data traffic secret stored in\n378\t    /// this connection's TLS context for the chosen direction.  C:\n379\t    /// `get_app_secret`.\n380\t    pub fn app_secret(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) -> &mut [u8] {\n381\t        todo!()\n382\t    }\n383\t\n384\t    /// Length (bytes) of the app-data traffic secret — the digest\n385\t    /// size of the negotiated cipher's hash.  C:\n386\t    /// `get_app_secret_size`.  Phase 3 may collapse this accessor\n387\t    /// since [`Connection::app_secret`] already returns a sized slice.\n388\t    pub fn app_secret_size(&self) -> usize {\n389\t        todo!()\n390\t    }\n391\t\n392\t    /// Compute the post-rotation AEAD + PN contexts and stash them\n393\t    /// in `crypto_context_new`.  C: `compute_new_rotated_keys`.\n394\t    pub fn compute_new_rotated_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n395\t        todo!()\n396\t    }\n397\t\n398\t    /// Promote `crypto_context_new` to the active\n399\t    /// `crypto_context[3]` slot, demoting the previous keys.  C:\n400\t    /// `apply_rotated_keys`.\n401\t    pub fn apply_rotated_keys(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) {\n402\t        todo!()\n403\t    }\n404\t}\n405\t\n406\t/// Rotate the application traffic secret in place using the\n407\t/// version-specific traffic-update label.  The active hash\n408\t/// algorithm is supplied through a fresh [`digest::DynDigest`]\n409\t/// instance (callers obtain one from\n410\t/// [`crate::tls::Session`]).  C: `rotate_app_secret`.\n411\tpub fn rotate_app_secret(\n412\t    _hash: &mut dyn digest::DynDigest,\n413\t    _secret: &mut [u8],\n414\t    _traffic_update_label: &str,\n415\t) -> Result<(), Error> {\n416\t    todo!()\n417\t}\n418\t\n419\timpl CryptoContext {\n420\t    /// Free every AEAD / PN-encryption slot held by this crypto\n421\t    /// context.  Called from key-rotation paths to recycle the\n422\t    /// underlying tls handles without dropping the parent\n423\t    /// [`Connection`], so this is *not* an `impl Drop` — Phase 3 may\n424\t    /// still install one for the parent-drop path.  C:\n425\t    /// `crypto_context_free`.\n426\t    pub fn free_handles(&mut self) {\n427\t        todo!()\n428\t    }\n429\t}\n430\t\n431\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n432\t// Test helpers (still part of the public API surface).\n433\t//\n434\t// These two helpers build standalone AEAD / PN-encryption contexts\n435\t// from a raw secret.  They're used by the test suite to mock\n436\t// crypto state.  The Phase 2 trait family ([`crate::tls`]) is the\n437\t// return shape.\n438\t\n439\t/// Construct an AEAD context backed by AES128-GCM-SHA256 from a\n440\t/// raw secret + prefix label.  C: `setup_test_aead_context`.\n441\tpub fn setup_test_aead_context(\n442\t    _is_encrypt: bool,\n443\t    _secret: &[u8],\n444\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n445\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n446\t    todo!()\n447\t}\n448\t\n449\t/// Construct a PN-encryption context for tests.  C:\n450\t/// `pn_enc_create_for_test`.\n451\tpub fn pn_enc_create_for_test(\n452\t    _secret: &[u8],\n453\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n454\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n455\t    todo!()\n456\t}\n457\t\n458\t/// Construct a header-protection cipher context directly from a raw\n459\t/// 16-byte AES-128 key (no HKDF derivation).  Used by `pn_ctr_test`\n460\t/// to verify the AES-128-ECB keystream against a known answer.\n461\t/// C: `ptls_cipher_new(aead->ctr_cipher, 1, key)`.\n462\tpub fn test_pn_enc_from_raw_key(_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n463\t    todo!()\n464\t}\n465\t\n466\t/// HKDF-Expand-Label (RFC 8446 §7.1) using the QUIC-specific label\n467\t/// encoding.  The output length is determined by `output.len()`.\n468\t/// Uses SHA-256 (the hash fixed for QUIC Initial-secret derivation).\n469\t/// C: `ptls_hkdf_expand_label(cipher->hash, output, output_len,\n470\t///    ptls_iovec(secret), label, empty_ctx, base_label)`.\n471\tpub fn hkdf_expand_label(\n472\t    _label: &str,\n473\t    _base_label: &str,\n474\t    _secret: &[u8],\n475\t    _output: &mut [u8],\n476\t) -> Result<(), crate::Error> {\n477\t    todo!()\n478\t}\n479\t\n480\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n481\t// Reset secret and verify-certificate management.\n482\t\n483\timpl Quic {\n484\t    /// Compute the 16-byte reset secret tied to `connection_id` using this\n485\t    /// context's reset seed.  C: `create_connection_id_reset_secret`.\n486\t    pub fn create_connection_id_reset_secret(\n487\t        &mut self,\n488\t        _cnx_id: &ConnectionId,\n489\t        _reset_secret: &mut [u8; RESET_SECRET_SIZE],\n490\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n491\t        todo!()\n492\t    }\n493\t\n494\t    // The C `tls_set_verify_certificate_callback` /\n495\t    // `dispose_verify_certificate_callback` pair is folded into\n496\t    // [`crate::tls::TlsCallbacks`] — applications install a single\n497\t    // callbacks bundle on the QUIC context and override\n498\t    // `verify_certificate` to plug in their own logic.\n499\t\n500\t    /// Toggle whether the server requires client certificates.  C\n501\t    /// took an `int`; promoted to `bool`.  C:\n502\t    /// `tls_set_client_authentication`.\n503\t    pub fn tls_set_client_authentication(&mut self, _client_authentication: bool) {\n504\t        todo!()\n505\t    }\n506\t\n507\t    /// Report whether client authentication is currently required.\n508\t    /// C: `tls_client_authentication_activated`.\n509\t    pub fn tls_client_authentication_activated(&self) -> bool {\n510\t        todo!()\n511\t    }\n512\t\n513\t    /// Toggle whether tls exposes its exporter API on this master\n514\t    /// context.  C: `tls_set_use_exporter`.\n515\t    pub fn tls_set_use_exporter(&mut self, _use_exporter: bool) {\n516\t        todo!()\n517\t    }\n518\t}\n519\t\n520\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n521\t// Retry tokens.\n522\t//\n523\t// `server_decrypt_retry_token`,\n524\t// `prepare_retry_token`, and\n525\t// `verify_retry_token` were the three token entry points\n526\t// declared in this header.  All three take a `struct sockaddr*`\n527\t// peer address (mapped to [`SocketAddr`]) and produce / consume a\n528\t// caller-allocated `token` buffer.\n529\t\n530\t/// Output bundle from [`server_decrypt_retry_token`].  C\n531\t/// returned the new-token flag through `int*` and the plaintext\n532\t/// length through `size_t*`; collapsing them into a struct keeps\n533\t/// the result one piece.  The plaintext is written into the\n534\t/// caller's `text` slice; `text_length` is how many bytes were\n535\t/// actually written.\n536\tpub struct DecryptedRetryToken {\n537\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token` out-parameter; promoted\n538\t    /// to `bool`.\n539\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n540\t    /// Mirrors the C `size_t* text_length` out-parameter.\n541\t    pub text_length: usize,\n542\t}\n543\t\n544\timpl Quic {\n545\t    /// Decrypt a retry token and verify its peer-address binding.\n546\t    /// The plaintext is written into `text`; the result describes\n547\t    /// how many bytes were written and whether the token was a \"new\n548\t    /// token\" or a classic retry token.  C:\n549\t    /// `server_decrypt_retry_token`.\n550\t    pub fn server_decrypt_retry_token(\n551\t        &mut self,\n552\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n553\t        _token: &[u8],\n554\t        _text: &mut [u8],\n555\t    ) -> Result<DecryptedRetryToken, Error> {\n556\t        todo!()\n557\t    }\n558\t\n559\t    /// Construct a retry / new token signed for `addr_peer`.\n560\t    /// Returns the number of bytes written into `token`; `token_max`\n561\t    /// is `token.len()`.  C: `prepare_retry_token`.\n562\t    pub fn prepare_retry_token(\n563\t        &mut self,\n564\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n565\t        _current_time: Instant,\n566\t        _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n567\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n568\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n569\t        _token: &mut [u8],\n570\t    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n571\t        todo!()\n572\t    }\n573\t}\n574\t\n575\t/// Output bundle from [`verify_retry_token`].  Mirrors the\n576\t/// `int* is_new_token` and `ConnectionId* odcid`\n577\t/// out-parameters of the C signature.\n578\tpub struct VerifiedRetryToken {\n579\t    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token`; promoted to `bool`.\n580\t    pub is_new_token: bool,\n581\t    /// Original Destination Connection ID extracted from the\n582\t    /// token.  Empty (`id_len == 0`) for \"new tokens\".\n583\t    pub odcid: ConnectionId,\n584\t}\n585\t\n586\timpl Quic {\n587\t    /// Verify a retry / new token presented by the peer.  Returns\n588\t    /// the extracted bundle when the token is fresh, address-bound,\n589\t    /// and (for retry tokens) RCID-matched.  C:\n590\t    /// `verify_retry_token`.\n591\t    pub fn verify_retry_token(\n592\t        &mut self,\n593\t        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n594\t        _current_time: Instant,\n595\t        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n596\t        _initial_pn: u32,\n597\t        _token: &[u8],\n598\t        _check_reuse: bool,\n599\t    ) -> Result<VerifiedRetryToken, Error> {\n600\t        todo!()\n601\t    }\n602\t}\n603\t\n604\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n605\t// Hash helpers exposed so applications don't need tls.h.\n606\t\n607\t/// Maximum digest size across the hash algorithms tls supports.\n608\t/// C: `HASH_SIZE_MAX`.\n609\tpub const HASH_SIZE_MAX: usize = 64;\n610\t\n611\t/// Construct a streaming hash context for the named algorithm\n612\t/// (e.g. `\"sha256\"`).  Returns a `digest::DynDigest` trait\n613\t/// object so callers feed bytes via `update` and read out via\n614\t/// `finalize` (the canonical Rust Crypto shape).\n615\t///\n616\t/// Backends supply concrete digest types (e.g. `sha2::Sha256`)\n617\t/// and lift them through this entry point.\n618\tpub fn hash_create(_algorithm_name: &str) -> Option<Box<dyn digest::DynDigest>> {\n619\t    todo!()\n620\t}\n621\t\n622\t/// Digest length (bytes) of the named hash algorithm, or 0 when\n623\t/// the algorithm is unknown.  C: `hash_get_length`.\n624\tpub fn hash_get_length(_algorithm_name: &str) -> usize {\n625\t    todo!()\n626\t}\n627\t\n628\t// `hash_update` and `hash_finalize` are gone -- callers use\n629\t// `digest::DynDigest`'s `update` and `finalize_into` methods on\n630\t// the boxed trait object returned by `hash_create`.\n631\t\n632\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n633\t// Private-key / certificate file loaders.\n634\t\n635\timpl Quic {\n636\t    /// Load a PEM-encoded private key from `file_name` and install\n637\t    /// it in this context's master TLS context.  C:\n638\t    /// `set_private_key_from_file`.\n639\t    pub fn set_private_key_from_file(&mut self, _file_name: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n640\t        todo!()\n641\t    }\n642\t}\n643\t\n644\t/// Load a PEM-encoded certificate chain from `file_name` and\n645\t/// return it as an owned vector of DER-encoded certificate byte\n646\t/// strings.  C: `get_certs_from_file` (which allocated both the\n647\t/// outer iovec array and each `base` slot — the Rust shape owns\n648\t/// both via the nested `Vec<Vec<u8>>`).  Returns `None` when the\n649\t/// loader callback is unset or the file fails to parse.\n650\tpub fn get_certs_from_file(_file_name: &str) -> Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>> {\n651\t    todo!()\n652\t}\n653\t\n654\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n655\t// Retry-packet integrity protection.\n656\t//\n657\t// These manage a small set of AEAD contexts (one per supported\n658\t// QUIC version) used to compute retry-packet integrity tags.\n659\t\n660\t/// Build a retry-protection AEAD context from the retry integrity\n661\t/// key for a given version.  C: `create_retry_protection_context`.\n662\tpub fn create_retry_protection_context(\n663\t    _is_enc: bool,\n664\t    _key: &[u8],\n665\t    _prefix_label: &str,\n666\t) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n667\t    todo!()\n668\t}\n669\t\n670\timpl Quic {\n671\t    /// Look up (and lazily create) the retry-protection context for\n672\t    /// the chosen QUIC version, on the chosen direction.  C:\n673\t    /// `find_retry_protection_context`.\n674\t    pub fn find_retry_protection_context(\n675\t        &mut self,\n676\t        _version_index: i32,\n677\t        _sending: bool,\n678\t    ) -> Option<&mut dyn crate::tls::PacketKey> {\n679\t        todo!()\n680\t    }\n681\t\n682\t    /// Tear down every retry-protection AEAD context held by this\n683\t    /// context.  C: `delete_retry_protection_contexts`.\n684\t    pub fn delete_retry_protection_contexts(&mut self) {\n685\t        todo!()\n686\t    }\n687\t}\n688\t\n689\t/// Append the integrity tag to the bytes already written into the\n690\t/// retry packet buffer.  Returns the new write index.  C:\n691\t/// `encode_retry_protection`.\n692\tpub fn encode_retry_protection(\n693\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n694\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n695\t    _byte_index: usize,\n696\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n697\t) -> usize {\n698\t    todo!()\n699\t}\n700\t\n701\t/// Verify the integrity tag at the end of an inbound retry packet.\n702\t/// Returns the new payload length (with the tag stripped).\n703\t/// C: `verify_retry_protection`.\n704\tpub fn verify_retry_protection(\n705\t    _integrity_aead: &dyn crate::tls::PacketKey,\n706\t    _bytes: &mut [u8],\n707\t    _length: usize,\n708\t    _byte_index: usize,\n709\t    _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n710\t) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n711\t    todo!()\n712\t}\n713\t\n714\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n715\t// Cipher-suite accessors and ECB cipher for CID encryption.\n716\t\n717\t// The cipher-suite lookup functions (`get_cipher_suite_by_id_v`,\n718\t// `get_aes128gcm_sha256_v`, `get_aes128gcm_v`, `ecb_create_by_name`)\n719\t// returned opaque `*mut c_void` cipher-suite handles in C.  In\n720\t// Phase 2 the cipher-suite registry lives on the\n721\t// `crate::tls::TlsBackend` and is reached through\n722\t// `Session::next_1rtt_keys` etc., which return typed\n723\t// `KeyPair`s.  The free-function lookups disappear.\n724\t\n725\t/// AES-128-ECB cipher context.  Phase 2: the C `*mut c_void`\n726\t/// wrapper is gone — this struct now owns a concrete\n727\t/// [`aes::Aes128`] (the Rust Crypto AES implementation).  Used\n728\t/// by the load-balancer CID-encryption flow in [`crate::lb`];\n729\t/// header protection has its own AES paths in\n730\t/// [`crate::header_protection`].\n731\t///\n732\t/// `Direction` distinguishes encrypt-mode from decrypt-mode (the\n733\t/// C side took an `is_enc` flag); each backing AES type\n734\t/// implements one direction only.\n735\tpub enum Aes128EcbContext {\n736\t    Encrypt(aes::Aes128Enc),\n737\t    Decrypt(aes::Aes128Dec),\n738\t}\n739\t\n740\timpl core::fmt::Debug for Aes128EcbContext {\n741\t    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut core::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> core::fmt::Result {\n742\t        f.debug_struct(\"Aes128EcbContext\").finish_non_exhaustive()\n743\t    }\n744\t}\n745\t\n746\timpl Aes128EcbContext {\n747\t    /// Construct an AES-128-ECB context (encrypt or decrypt) keyed\n748\t    /// by `ecb_key`.  C: `aes128_ecb_create`.\n749\t    pub fn new(_is_enc: bool, _ecb_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Self {\n750\t        todo!()\n751\t    }\n752\t\n753\t    /// Encrypt or decrypt `block` in place.  C:\n754\t    /// `aes128_ecb_encrypt` (encrypt-mode only — decrypt-mode\n755\t    /// is exposed through the same method here, since the\n756\t    /// direction is fixed at construction).\n757\t    pub fn process(&self, _block: &mut [u8; 16]) {\n758\t        todo!()\n759\t    }\n760\t}\n761\t\n762\t// The C `tls_api_init` / `tls_api_unload` / `tls_api_reset` family\n763\t// loaded picotls' optional providers (OpenSSL / minicrypto / fusion\n764\t// / mbedtls) into a global registry.  The Rust shape is direct\n765\t// backend selection: the application picks an implementation of\n766\t// [`crate::tls::TlsBackend`] (e.g. `crate::sys::picotls::Picotls`)\n767\t// and hands it to the QUIC context.  No global init step.\n768\t//\n769\t// `tls_api_log_versions` likewise disappears — the active backend\n770\t// owns its own version-string surface.\n771\t\n772\t#[cfg(test)]\n773\tmod test {}\n774\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"e04086e8-c39f-47ef-b6d3-d6f79b3b1b34","timestamp":"2026-05-05T17:01:54.394Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tls_api.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/tls_api.h`.\n//!\n//! `tls_api.h` is the boundary between quic-core's QUIC machinery\n//! and the TLS 1.3 stack underneath.  It exposes the master\n//! TLS context lifecycle, per-connection TLS contexts, the\n//! retry-token / retry-protection helpers, and a handful of\n//! provider-installation entry points kept in this header so\n//! applications don't have to include `tls.h`.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//! Phase 4 fills in the bodies.\n//!\n//! ## Shape conventions\n//!\n//! * Free C functions whose first argument is a `Quic*` or `Connection*`\n//!   become inherent methods on [`Quic`] / [`Connection`].\n//! * The C `void*` AEAD / PN-encryption / cipher / hash contexts\n//!   are gone — Phase 2 replaced them with the trait family in\n//!   [`crate::tls`] (`PacketKey`, `HeaderKey`, `Session`,\n//!   `ClientConfig`, `ServerConfig`).  Backend implementations\n//!   (picotls, rustls, OpenSSL) wrap their native session types\n//!   to satisfy those traits.  No `unsafe fn` or raw-pointer\n//!   parameters remain in this module.\n//! * `int` flag parameters that encode booleans (`is_enc`,\n//!   `is_client`, `client_mode`, `use_low_memory`, `reset`,\n//!   `check_reuse`, `sending`) become `bool`.\n//! * C status-code returns (`int` 0/-1 / `ERROR_*`) become\n//!   `Result<T, Error>`.  The `SIZE_MAX` failure sentinel returned\n//!   by `aead_decrypt_*` becomes `Err(Error::AeadCheck)` instead of\n//!   leaking the sentinel through the success arm.\n//! * Owning `PtlsIovec* get_certs_from_file(…, size_t* count)`\n//!   collapses to `Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>>`: the C callee `malloc`s\n//!   the slot array and writes its length through the out-pointer,\n//!   and the caller `free`s.  A Rust `Vec` carries both ownership\n//!   and length, with `Drop` taking the place of the manual free.\n//! * Output parameters (`uint8_t reset_secret[16]`,\n//!   `uint8_t* master_secret`, `void** aead_ctx`, `size_t*\n//!   text_length`, `int* data_consumed`, `int* is_new_token`,\n//!   `ConnectionId* odcid`, `size_t* token_size`, `size_t* length`)\n//!   become `&mut`-borrowed slots or are returned as part of the\n//!   function's result tuple, depending on whether the caller\n//!   already holds storage.\n//! * `struct sockaddr*` arguments map to [`core::net::SocketAddr`]\n//!   for parity with `quic.rs`.\n//! * `FreeVerifyCertificateCtx` is a function-pointer typedef in\n//!   `quic.h`; the trait counterpart is already in `quic.rs` and is\n//!   reused here.\n//! * Function-pointer parameters in callbacks\n//!   ([`Quic::tls_set_verify_certificate_callback`]) take\n//!   `Option<Box<dyn …>>` — the C `NULL` sentinel maps to `None`\n//!   and the registered callback is owned by the registry.\n//!\n//! ## Initial-secret label constants\n//!\n//! `LABEL_QUIC_BASE` is `#define`d as `NULL` in C — it's the \"no\n//! prefix label\" sentinel passed to tls.  In Rust the prefix-label\n//! parameters that accept it become `Option<&str>`, so the constant\n//! itself doesn't appear; the call sites pass `None` when they would\n//! have passed `LABEL_QUIC_BASE`.\n//!\n//! ## Public vs. internal `set_*` methods\n//!\n//! `picoquic.h` exposes `picoquic_set_verify_certificate_callback`,\n//! `picoquic_set_client_authentication`, and\n//! `picoquic_set_use_exporter` as thin wrappers around the\n//! `picoquic_tls_set_*` functions declared here.  The wrappers live\n//! on [`Quic`] without the `tls_` prefix (see `lib.rs`); the\n//! TLS-side methods in this module keep the `tls_` prefix so both\n//! pairs can coexist.\n//!\n//! ## Out of scope\n//!\n//! * The five `#if 0`-disabled `cid_*_under_mask_ctx` /\n//!   `cid_free_encrypt_global_ctx` entries are dead code in the C\n//!   source and are not translated.\n//! * `get_private_key_from_file` is also `#if 0` in the header (and\n//!   the `_t` callback variant lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`);\n//!   not translated.\n\nextern crate alloc;\n\nuse alloc::boxed::Box;\nuse alloc::vec::Vec;\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::Instant;\nuse crate::internal::CryptoContext;\nuse crate::{Connection, ConnectionId, Error, Quic, RESET_SECRET_SIZE};\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Label constants (C `#define` → `&str`).\n//\n// All labels are NUL-terminated bytes in the C header because tls\n// takes them as `char const*`.  The Rust shape is plain `&str`; the\n// trailing NUL is reintroduced (if needed) at the tls FFI\n// boundary.\n\n/// Initial secret label for the client direction.  C:\n/// `LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT`.\npub const LABEL_INITIAL_CLIENT: &str = \"client in\";\n\n/// Initial secret label for the server direction.  C:\n/// `LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER`.\npub const LABEL_INITIAL_SERVER: &str = \"server in\";\n\n/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v1.  C:\n/// `LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\npub const LABEL_V1_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quic ku\";\n\n/// Traffic-update label for QUIC v2.  C:\n/// `LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE`.\npub const LABEL_V2_TRAFFIC_UPDATE: &str = \"quicv2 ku\";\n\n/// HKDF \"key\" component label.  C: `LABEL_KEY`.\npub const LABEL_KEY: &str = \"key\";\n\n/// HKDF \"iv\" component label.  C: `LABEL_IV`.\npub const LABEL_IV: &str = \"iv\";\n\n/// HKDF \"hp\" (header protection) component label.  C:\n/// `LABEL_HP`.\npub const LABEL_HP: &str = \"hp\";\n\n/// HKDF \"cid\" component label.  C: `LABEL_CID`.\npub const LABEL_CID: &str = \"cid\";\n\n/// HKDF label for the global CID encryption secret.  C:\n/// `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL`.\npub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL: &str = \"cid global\";\n\n/// Number of HKDF rounds run when deriving the global CID secret.\n/// C: `LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS`.\npub const LABEL_CID_GLOBAL_ROUNDS: u32 = 4;\n\n/// QUIC v1 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n/// `LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE`.\npub const LABEL_QUIC_V1_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quic \";\n\n/// QUIC v2 TLS key-derivation label prefix.  C:\n/// `LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE`.\npub const LABEL_QUIC_V2_KEY_BASE: &str = \"tls13 quicv2 \";\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Master TLS context.\n//\n// In Phase 2 the C `*mut c_void tls_master_ctx` is replaced by\n// `Quic.tls_client_config` / `tls_server_config` (boxed\n// `DynClientConfig` / `DynServerConfig` trait objects defined in\n// [`crate::tls`]).  Both lifecycle entry points install or\n// release state on the QUIC context itself.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Initialize this context's master TLS context.  Loads the\n    /// certificate chain, the private key, the trusted-root bundle\n    /// and the ticket-encryption key.  Any of the file-name / key\n    /// parameters may be `NULL` in C (server-only, no roots, no\n    /// ticket key); Rust models that with `Option<&str>` /\n    /// `Option<&[u8]>`.  C: `master_tlscontext`.\n    pub fn init_master_tls_context(\n        &mut self,\n        _cert_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _key_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _cert_root_file_name: Option<&str>,\n        _ticket_key: Option<&[u8]>,\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Tear down the master TLS context installed by\n    /// [`Quic::init_master_tls_context`].  C:\n    /// `master_tlscontext_free`.\n    pub fn free_master_tls_context(&mut self) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Per-connection TLS context.\n//\n// `tls_ctx_t` itself lives in `crypto_provider_api.rs`; this module\n// just exposes the API surface that quic-core uses to drive it.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Allocate a per-connection TLS context, attach it to this\n    /// connection, and initialize the tls handshake-property slots.\n    /// C: `tlscontext_create`.  C side returns 0 on success,\n    /// `ERROR_TLS_SERVER_CON_WITHOUT_CERT` / `ERROR_MEMORY` / -1 on\n    /// failure.\n    pub fn create_tls_context(&mut self, _quic: &mut Quic) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Drop transient buffers (ALPN list, transport-parameter encode\n    /// scratch) once the handshake is done.  C:\n    /// `tlscontext_trim_after_handshake`.\n    pub fn trim_tls_context_after_handshake(&mut self) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Forget the session ticket installed for a 0-RTT attempt.  C:\n    /// `tlscontext_remove_ticket`.\n    pub fn remove_tls_ticket(&mut self) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// `tls_context_free` is gone -- `Connection.tls_ctx` is now an\n// `Option<Box<dyn Session>>`, and `Drop` on the boxed Session\n// runs the backend's teardown automatically.  The C\n// `client_mode` flag is unnecessary too: the `Session` impl\n// knows whether it's a client or server session internally.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// TLS stream processing.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Drive the TLS handshake by feeding in any data buffered on\n    /// the crypto streams and pushing produced data back out.\n    /// Returns the number of bytes consumed (the C `int*\n    /// data_consumed` out-parameter; bytes consumed is non-negative,\n    /// so the type widens to `usize` rather than tracking the C\n    /// `int`).  C: `tls_stream_process`.\n    pub fn process_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Report whether the TLS handshake has completed.  C signature\n    /// returned `int` (0/1); promoted to `bool`.  C:\n    /// `is_tls_complete`.\n    pub fn is_tls_complete(&self) -> bool {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Send the initial `ClientHello` (or the response to a\n    /// `HelloRetry`) on the TLS stream.  C:\n    /// `initialize_tls_stream`.\n    pub fn initialize_tls_stream(&mut self, _current_time: Instant) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Read the virtual time tls sees through its `get_time`\n    /// callback (microseconds).  C: `get_tls_time`.\n    pub fn tls_time(&self) -> u64 {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Random number generation.\n//\n// The crypto-grade RNG is the tls / OpenSSL provider; the public RNG\n// is xorshift1024* seeded from the crypto RNG.  Both take a\n// destination slice; the C `(void* buf, size_t len)` pair collapses\n// to `&mut [u8]`.\n\n// `crypto_random` / `crypto_uniform_random` / `seed_public_random`\n// are gone -- callers reach `Quic.rng` (a `Box<dyn CryptoRng>`)\n// directly and use the `rand::Rng` / `rand::RngCore` methods on\n// it.  The public-RNG helpers (`public_random_64` etc.) likewise\n// disappear: callers use `rand::rng()` for the thread-local\n// non-secure stream.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// AEAD primitives.\n//\n// The C `aead_*` family wrapped opaque `void*` handles around\n// `ptls_aead_context_t*`.  Phase 2 replaced them with the\n// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] trait — backends supply implementations,\n// the rest of the crate is generic over them.  This module\n// retains only the constant `QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN` (the fixed\n// 16-byte tag QUIC mandates).\n\n/// Length of the QUIC AEAD authentication tag.  All TLS 1.3\n/// cipher suites used by QUIC (RFC 9001 §5.3) produce a 16-byte\n/// tag, so the value is fixed.  Replaces the C\n/// `aead_get_checksum_length(void*)` accessor; once a\n/// [`crate::tls::PacketKey`] exists, prefer `key.tag_len()`.\npub const QUIC_AEAD_TAG_LEN: usize = 16;\n\n// The AEAD wrappers (`aead_encrypt_generic`, `aead_decrypt_generic`,\n// `aead_encrypt_mp` / `aead_decrypt_mp`, `aead_integrity_limit`,\n// `aead_confidentiality_limit`) are gone -- their behaviour is the\n// `crate::tls::PacketKey` trait.  Backends supply concrete\n// implementations.  Multipath variants fold into the same trait by\n// XORing the path id into the nonce inside the implementor.\n//\n// `aead_free` and `cipher_free` are gone -- `Drop` on the boxed\n// trait object replaces them.\n//\n// `pn_iv_size` and `pn_encrypt` are gone -- header protection\n// (the only consumer) is implemented in `crate::header_protection`\n// and exposed through `crate::tls::HeaderKey`.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Initial-secret derivation.\n//\n// RFC 9001 fixes the initial-secret HKDF to SHA-256 (the salt is the\n// version-specific 20-byte constant).  Both helpers therefore commit\n// to SHA-256 internally — no cipher-suite parameter needed (the C\n// API took `&PtlsCipherSuite` purely for parity with later epoch\n// derivations).  `master_secret` / `client_secret` / `server_secret`\n// must be 32 bytes (`Sha256::output_size()`); a wider buffer is\n// fine (caller-provided slots stay flexible).\n\n/// Derive the per-connection-ID initial master secret.  C:\n/// `setup_initial_master_secret`.\npub fn setup_initial_master_secret(\n    _salt: &[u8],\n    _initial_connection_id: ConnectionId,\n    _master_secret: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Derive client/server initial secrets from the master secret.\n/// `client_secret` and `server_secret` are filled in place.  C:\n/// `setup_initial_secrets`.\npub fn setup_initial_secrets(\n    _master_secret: &[u8],\n    _client_secret: &mut [u8],\n    _server_secret: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Set up this connection's per-epoch initial AEAD / PN\n    /// encryption contexts from the connection's initial CID.  C:\n    /// `setup_initial_traffic_keys`.\n    pub fn setup_initial_traffic_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Output bundle from [`Quic::initial_aead_context`].  C returned\n/// the AEAD and PN contexts through `void**` out-pointers; the Rust\n/// shape collapses both into a struct so the function signature is\n/// one-out, one-return.\npub struct InitialAeadContext {\n    /// Owned AEAD context for packet protection.\n    pub aead_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>,\n    /// Owned header-protection context.\n    pub pn_enc_ctx: Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Derive an AEAD + PN context for the initial encryption\n    /// level.  `version_index` selects the QUIC version's salt and\n    /// prefix label; `is_client` and `is_enc` pick the\n    /// client/server and encrypt/decrypt directions.  C:\n    /// `get_initial_aead_context`.\n    pub fn initial_aead_context(\n        &mut self,\n        _version_index: i32,\n        _initial_connection_id: &ConnectionId,\n        _is_client: bool,\n        _is_enc: bool,\n    ) -> Result<InitialAeadContext, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Application-secret access and rotation.\n//\n// `get_app_secret` returns one of the two\n// `app_secret_*` buffers stashed inside `tls_ctx_t`.  The\n// C signature returned a `uint8_t*` into that buffer; in Rust the\n// natural shape is a borrowed slice carrying the digest length\n// (callers consult `get_app_secret_size` for the length\n// today, which is redundant once the borrow encodes it).  Phase 3\n// may collapse the size accessor.\n\nimpl Connection {\n    /// Return a borrow of the app-data traffic secret stored in\n    /// this connection's TLS context for the chosen direction.  C:\n    /// `get_app_secret`.\n    pub fn app_secret(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) -> &mut [u8] {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Length (bytes) of the app-data traffic secret — the digest\n    /// size of the negotiated cipher's hash.  C:\n    /// `get_app_secret_size`.  Phase 3 may collapse this accessor\n    /// since [`Connection::app_secret`] already returns a sized slice.\n    pub fn app_secret_size(&self) -> usize {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Compute the post-rotation AEAD + PN contexts and stash them\n    /// in `crypto_context_new`.  C: `compute_new_rotated_keys`.\n    pub fn compute_new_rotated_keys(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Promote `crypto_context_new` to the active\n    /// `crypto_context[3]` slot, demoting the previous keys.  C:\n    /// `apply_rotated_keys`.\n    pub fn apply_rotated_keys(&mut self, _is_enc: bool) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Rotate the application traffic secret in place using the\n/// version-specific traffic-update label.  The active hash\n/// algorithm is supplied through a fresh [`digest::DynDigest`]\n/// instance (callers obtain one from\n/// [`crate::tls::Session`]).  C: `rotate_app_secret`.\npub fn rotate_app_secret(\n    _hash: &mut dyn digest::DynDigest,\n    _secret: &mut [u8],\n    _traffic_update_label: &str,\n) -> Result<(), Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\nimpl CryptoContext {\n    /// Free every AEAD / PN-encryption slot held by this crypto\n    /// context.  Called from key-rotation paths to recycle the\n    /// underlying tls handles without dropping the parent\n    /// [`Connection`], so this is *not* an `impl Drop` — Phase 3 may\n    /// still install one for the parent-drop path.  C:\n    /// `crypto_context_free`.\n    pub fn free_handles(&mut self) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Test helpers (still part of the public API surface).\n//\n// These two helpers build standalone AEAD / PN-encryption contexts\n// from a raw secret.  They're used by the test suite to mock\n// crypto state.  The Phase 2 trait family ([`crate::tls`]) is the\n// return shape.\n\n/// Construct an AEAD context backed by AES128-GCM-SHA256 from a\n/// raw secret + prefix label.  C: `setup_test_aead_context`.\npub fn setup_test_aead_context(\n    _is_encrypt: bool,\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Construct a PN-encryption context for tests.  C:\n/// `pn_enc_create_for_test`.\npub fn pn_enc_create_for_test(\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Construct a header-protection cipher context directly from a raw\n/// 16-byte AES-128 key (no HKDF derivation).  Used by `pn_ctr_test`\n/// to verify the AES-128-ECB keystream against a known answer.\n/// C: `ptls_cipher_new(aead->ctr_cipher, 1, key)`.\npub fn test_pn_enc_from_raw_key(_key: &[u8; 16]) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::HeaderKey>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// HKDF-Expand-Label (RFC 8446 §7.1) using the QUIC-specific label\n/// encoding.  The output length is determined by `output.len()`.\n/// Uses SHA-256 (the hash fixed for QUIC Initial-secret derivation).\n/// C: `ptls_hkdf_expand_label(cipher->hash, output, output_len,\n///    ptls_iovec(secret), label, empty_ctx, base_label)`.\npub fn hkdf_expand_label(\n    _label: &str,\n    _base_label: &str,\n    _secret: &[u8],\n    _output: &mut [u8],\n) -> Result<(), crate::Error> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Reset secret and verify-certificate management.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Compute the 16-byte reset secret tied to `connection_id` using this\n    /// context's reset seed.  C: `create_connection_id_reset_secret`.\n    pub fn create_connection_id_reset_secret(\n        &mut self,\n        _cnx_id: &ConnectionId,\n        _reset_secret: &mut [u8; RESET_SECRET_SIZE],\n    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    // The C `tls_set_verify_certificate_callback` /\n    // `dispose_verify_certificate_callback` pair is folded into\n    // [`crate::tls::TlsCallbacks`] — applications install a single\n    // callbacks bundle on the QUIC context and override\n    // `verify_certificate` to plug in their own logic.\n\n    /// Toggle whether the server requires client certificates.  C\n    /// took an `int`; promoted to `bool`.  C:\n    /// `tls_set_client_authentication`.\n    pub fn tls_set_client_authentication(&mut self, _client_authentication: bool) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Report whether client authentication is currently required.\n    /// C: `tls_client_authentication_activated`.\n    pub fn tls_client_authentication_activated(&self) -> bool {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Toggle whether tls exposes its exporter API on this master\n    /// context.  C: `tls_set_use_exporter`.\n    pub fn tls_set_use_exporter(&mut self, _use_exporter: bool) {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Retry tokens.\n//\n// `server_decrypt_retry_token`,\n// `prepare_retry_token`, and\n// `verify_retry_token` were the three token entry points\n// declared in this header.  All three take a `struct sockaddr*`\n// peer address (mapped to [`SocketAddr`]) and produce / consume a\n// caller-allocated `token` buffer.\n\n/// Output bundle from [`server_decrypt_retry_token`].  C\n/// returned the new-token flag through `int*` and the plaintext\n/// length through `size_t*`; collapsing them into a struct keeps\n/// the result one piece.  The plaintext is written into the\n/// caller's `text` slice; `text_length` is how many bytes were\n/// actually written.\npub struct DecryptedRetryToken {\n    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token` out-parameter; promoted\n    /// to `bool`.\n    pub is_new_token: bool,\n    /// Mirrors the C `size_t* text_length` out-parameter.\n    pub text_length: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Decrypt a retry token and verify its peer-address binding.\n    /// The plaintext is written into `text`; the result describes\n    /// how many bytes were written and whether the token was a \"new\n    /// token\" or a classic retry token.  C:\n    /// `server_decrypt_retry_token`.\n    pub fn server_decrypt_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _token: &[u8],\n        _text: &mut [u8],\n    ) -> Result<DecryptedRetryToken, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n\n    /// Construct a retry / new token signed for `addr_peer`.\n    /// Returns the number of bytes written into `token`; `token_max`\n    /// is `token.len()`.  C: `prepare_retry_token`.\n    pub fn prepare_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _odcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _initial_pn: u32,\n        _token: &mut [u8],\n    ) -> Result<usize, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Output bundle from [`verify_retry_token`].  Mirrors the\n/// `int* is_new_token` and `ConnectionId* odcid`\n/// out-parameters of the C signature.\npub struct VerifiedRetryToken {\n    /// Mirrors the C `int* is_new_token`; promoted to `bool`.\n    pub is_new_token: bool,\n    /// Original Destination Connection ID extracted from the\n    /// token.  Empty (`id_len == 0`) for \"new tokens\".\n    pub odcid: ConnectionId,\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Verify a retry / new token presented by the peer.  Returns\n    /// the extracted bundle when the token is fresh, address-bound,\n    /// and (for retry tokens) RCID-matched.  C:\n    /// `verify_retry_token`.\n    pub fn verify_retry_token(\n        &mut self,\n        _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n        _current_time: Instant,\n        _rcid: &ConnectionId,\n        _initial_pn: u32,\n        _token: &[u8],\n        _check_reuse: bool,\n    ) -> Result<VerifiedRetryToken, Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Hash helpers exposed so applications don't need tls.h.\n\n/// Maximum digest size across the hash algorithms tls supports.\n/// C: `HASH_SIZE_MAX`.\npub const HASH_SIZE_MAX: usize = 64;\n\n/// Construct a streaming hash context for the named algorithm\n/// (e.g. `\"sha256\"`).  Returns a `digest::DynDigest` trait\n/// object so callers feed bytes via `update` and read out via\n/// `finalize` (the canonical Rust Crypto shape).\n///\n/// Backends supply concrete digest types (e.g. `sha2::Sha256`)\n/// and lift them through this entry point.\npub fn hash_create(_algorithm_name: &str) -> Option<Box<dyn digest::DynDigest>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n/// Digest length (bytes) of the named hash algorithm, or 0 when\n/// the algorithm is unknown.  C: `hash_get_length`.\npub fn hash_get_length(_algorithm_name: &str) -> usize {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// `hash_update` and `hash_finalize` are gone -- callers use\n// `digest::DynDigest`'s `update` and `finalize_into` methods on\n// the boxed trait object returned by `hash_create`.\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Private-key / certificate file loaders.\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Load a PEM-encoded private key from `file_name` and install\n    /// it in this context's master TLS context.  C:\n    /// `set_private_key_from_file`.\n    pub fn set_private_key_from_file(&mut self, _file_name: &str) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n/// Load a PEM-encoded certificate chain from `file_name` and\n/// return it as an owned vector of DER-encoded certificate byte\n/// strings.  C: `get_certs_from_file` (which allocated both the\n/// outer iovec array and each `base` slot — the Rust shape owns\n/// both via the nested `Vec<Vec<u8>>`).  Returns `None` when the\n/// loader callback is unset or the file fails to parse.\npub fn get_certs_from_file(_file_name: &str) -> Option<Vec<Vec<u8>>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Retry-packet integrity protection.\n//\n// These manage a small set of AEAD contexts (one per supported\n// QUIC version) used to compute retry-packet integrity tags.\n\n/// Build a retry-protection AEAD context from the retry integrity\n/// key for a given version.  C: `create_retry_protection_context`.\npub fn create_retry_protection_context(\n    _is_enc: bool,\n    _key: &[u8],\n    _prefix_label: &str,\n) -> Option<Box<dyn crate::tls::PacketKey>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\nimpl Quic {\n    /// Look up (and lazily create