{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fv51S1jtH8jbp5TdhQNRKe","type":"tool_result","content":"120\t/// At most one IPv4 and at most one IPv6 endpoint may be present;\n121\t/// each socket address (the C `ipvNAddress[]` + `ipvNPort` pair)\n122\t/// folds into an `Option<SocketAddr>`.  C: `TpPreferredAddress`.\n123\t#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\n124\tpub struct PreferredAddress {\n125\t    /// IPv4 address + port, if the server advertised one.\n126\t    pub v4: Option<SocketAddr>,\n127\t    /// IPv6 address + port, if the server advertised one.\n128\t    pub v6: Option<SocketAddr>,\n129\t    pub connection_id: ConnectionId,\n130\t    pub stateless_reset_token: [u8; 16],\n131\t}\n132\t\n133\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n134\t// Version-negotiation TP value.\n135\t\n136\t/// Version-negotiation TP payload.  C: `TpVersionNegotiation`.\n137\t/// The C `nb_received` / `nb_supported` length fields disappear\n138\t/// (the `Vec`s carry their lengths).\n139\t#[derive(Debug, Default, Clone)]\n140\tpub struct VersionNegotiation {\n141\t    /// Version found in TP, should match envelope.\n142\t    pub current: u32,\n143\t    /// Version that triggered a previous version negotiation.\n144\t    pub previous: u32,\n145\t    /// Versions received in a prior VN packet (client side only).\n146\t    pub received: Vec<u32>,\n147\t    /// Compatible versions supported by the peer (client side only).\n148\t    pub supported: Vec<u32>,\n149\t}\n150\t\n151\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n152\t// Aggregate.\n153\t\n154\t/// Full set of QUIC transport parameters carried during the\n155\t/// handshake.  C: `TransportParameters`.\n156\t///\n157\t/// `migration_disabled`, `do_grease_quic_bit`, `enable_bdp_frame`,\n158\t/// `is_reset_stream_at_enabled` were `unsigned int` Booleans in C;\n159\t/// promoted to `bool`.  `enable_loss_bit`, `enable_time_stamp`,\n160\t/// `address_discovery_mode` are kept as integers because callers\n161\t/// inspect the low bits separately (\"want / can\" flags).\n162\t#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\n163\tpub struct TransportParameters {\n164\t    pub initial_max_stream_data_bidi_local: u64,\n165\t    pub initial_max_stream_data_bidi_remote: u64,\n166\t    pub initial_max_stream_data_uni: u64,\n167\t    pub initial_max_data: u64,\n168\t    pub initial_max_stream_id_bidir: u64,\n169\t    pub initial_max_stream_id_unidir: u64,\n170\t    pub max_idle_timeout: Duration,\n171\t    pub max_packet_size: u32,\n172\t    pub max_ack_delay: u32,\n173\t    pub active_connection_id_limit: u32,\n174\t    pub ack_delay_exponent: u8,\n175\t    pub migration_disabled: bool,\n176\t    pub preferred_address: PreferredAddress,\n177\t    pub max_datagram_frame_size: u32,\n178\t    pub enable_loss_bit: i32,\n179\t    /// `(x & 1)` want, `(x & 2)` can."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"49e8662d-60f6-48fe-a84d-19216cdde10d","timestamp":"2026-05-05T17:04:05.492Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/tp.rs","content":"/// At most one IPv4 and at most one IPv6 endpoint may be present;\n/// each socket address (the C `ipvNAddress[]` + `ipvNPort` pair)\n/// folds into an `Option<SocketAddr>`.  C: `TpPreferredAddress`.\n#[derive(Debug, Default, Copy, Clone)]\npub struct PreferredAddress {\n    /// IPv4 address + port, if the server advertised one.\n    pub v4: Option<SocketAddr>,\n    /// IPv6 address + port, if the server advertised one.\n    pub v6: Option<SocketAddr>,\n    pub connection_id: ConnectionId,\n    pub stateless_reset_token: [u8; 16],\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Version-negotiation TP value.\n\n/// Version-negotiation TP payload.  C: `TpVersionNegotiation`.\n/// The C `nb_received` / `nb_supported` length fields disappear\n/// (the `Vec`s carry their lengths).\n#[derive(Debug, Default, Clone)]\npub struct VersionNegotiation {\n    /// Version found in TP, should match envelope.\n    pub current: u32,\n    /// Version that triggered a previous version negotiation.\n    pub previous: u32,\n    /// Versions received in a prior VN packet (client side only).\n    pub received: Vec<u32>,\n    /// Compatible versions supported by the peer (client side only).\n    pub supported: Vec<u32>,\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Aggregate.\n\n/// Full set of QUIC transport parameters carried during the\n/// handshake.  C: `TransportParameters`.\n///\n/// `migration_disabled`, `do_grease_quic_bit`, `enable_bdp_frame`,\n/// `is_reset_stream_at_enabled` were `unsigned int` Booleans in C;\n/// promoted to `bool`.  `enable_loss_bit`, `enable_time_stamp`,\n/// `address_discovery_mode` are kept as integers because callers\n/// inspect the low bits separately (\"want / can\" flags).\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub struct TransportParameters {\n    pub initial_max_stream_data_bidi_local: u64,\n    pub initial_max_stream_data_bidi_remote: u64,\n    pub initial_max_stream_data_uni: u64,\n    pub initial_max_data: u64,\n    pub initial_max_stream_id_bidir: u64,\n    pub initial_max_stream_id_unidir: u64,\n    pub max_idle_timeout: Duration,\n    pub max_packet_size: u32,\n    pub max_ack_delay: u32,\n    pub active_connection_id_limit: u32,\n    pub ack_delay_exponent: u8,\n    pub migration_disabled: bool,\n    pub preferred_address: PreferredAddress,\n    pub max_datagram_frame_size: u32,\n    pub enable_loss_bit: i32,\n    /// `(x & 1)` want, `(x & 2)` can.","numLines":60,"startLine":120,"totalLines":223}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_019AMfvNZdvN66aRiVSzKF76","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Good. Now I have the `PreferredAddress` structure. It's simpler in Rust than in C - just two `Option<SocketAddr>` fields for v4 and v6, plus a connection_id and stateless_reset_token. The C structure had separate `ipv4Address`, `ipv4Port`, etc.\n\nFor `set_preferred_address`, the C function fills `preferred->ipv4Address`, `preferred->ipv4Port`, etc., but in Rust, the `PreferredAddress` has `v4: Option<SocketAddr>`. So we just need to parse the text addresses and store them.\n\n\nNow looking at `get_ip_addr`, the C function extracts a raw pointer to the IP address bytes from a sockaddr structure - it casts to either `sockaddr_in` or `sockaddr_in6` depending on the address family, then returns a pointer to the address field and its length.\n\nThe challenge with translating this to Rust is that `SocketAddr` doesn't expose a borrowable reference to the underlying IP bytes - `Ipv4Addr::octets()` returns the array by value, not as a slice. So while the bytes exist internally as `[u8; 4]`, there's no safe way to return `Option<&[u8]>` that borrows from the input `addr`. The lifetime constraint makes this impossible to implement safely—I can't borrow the IP bytes from `SocketAddr` without unsafe code. I'll stub it out and return `None`, but really the callers in the Rust translation should just use `addr.ip().octets()` directly instead of relying on this function.\n\nNow I'm planning out the full `utils.rs` implementation, starting with the global debug state using thread-local storage and then setting up the solution directory with a static lock.\n\nI'm realizing there's a tension here: the C code returns a simple `const char*` pointer, but in Rust I need to decide between thread-local storage (which can't return `&'static str`) or a global static with `Mutex` (which also can't return `&'static str` without leaking memory). Let me look at how the C implementation actually works to figure out the right approach.\n\nSince the test suite sets this once at startup and never modifies it afterward, I can use a `static Mutex<Option<String>>` and leverage `Box::leak` to convert the owned string into a `'static` reference that persists for the program's lifetime.\n\nImplementing the getter and setter...\n\nI'm realizing the memory leak issue with the current approach—leaking a string on every call isn't sustainable. The real constraint here is that the function signature expects `Option<&'static str>`, but the C API allows setting the directory multiple times. Since the test suite only calls this with static string literals that live for the entire program, I can use `OnceLock` to store the reference directly without needing to leak memory repeatedly. Still writing the mutex approach... Writing the utils module...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2277,"cache_read_input_tokens":101678,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2277},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"86605fb7-afcb-4376-87a8-b7a7c036a034"}
