{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019d6TjuTUNQ1Sr2CYh1hHtg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[package]\n2\tname = \"fq\"\n3\tversion = \"0.1.0\"\n4\tedition = \"2024\"\n5\t\n6\t[features]\n7\tdefault = []\n8\t# Each backend lives behind its own feature so a binary only pays\n9\t# for the implementations it actually links.  See `src/sys/`.\n10\tsys-picotls = []\n11\tsys-openssl = [\"dep:openssl\"]\n12\tsys-mbedtls = []\n13\tsys-fusion = []\n14\t\n15\t[dependencies]\n16\tfugit = { version = \"0.3\", default-features = false }\n17\trand = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\n18\trand_core = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\n19\t\n20\t# Rust Crypto trait crates (no implementations -- those come from\n21\t# the active TLS backend's crypto layer, except for header\n22\t# protection which we implement ourselves).\n23\taead = { version = \"0.5\", default-features = false }\n24\tcipher = { version = \"0.4\", default-features = false }\n25\tdigest = { version = \"0.10\", default-features = false }\n26\t\n27\t# Concrete cipher implementations used by header protection\n28\t# (QUIC packet-number encryption is QUIC logic, not TLS).\n29\taes = { version = \"0.8\", default-features = false }\n30\tchacha20 = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\n31\t\n32\t# UDP socket abstraction.  std-only.\n33\tsocket2 = { version = \"0.5\", default-features = false, features = [\"all\"] }\n34\t\n35\t# Optional backend implementations (gated by `sys-*` features).\n36\topenssl = { version = \"0.10\", optional = true }\n37\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"4794745b-2a3e-4f0b-86e7-cf4ec5943a71","timestamp":"2026-05-05T17:05:59.220Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/Cargo.toml","content":"[package]\nname = \"fq\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[features]\ndefault = []\n# Each backend lives behind its own feature so a binary only pays\n# for the implementations it actually links.  See `src/sys/`.\nsys-picotls = []\nsys-openssl = [\"dep:openssl\"]\nsys-mbedtls = []\nsys-fusion = []\n\n[dependencies]\nfugit = { version = \"0.3\", default-features = false }\nrand = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\nrand_core = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\n\n# Rust Crypto trait crates (no implementations -- those come from\n# the active TLS backend's crypto layer, except for header\n# protection which we implement ourselves).\naead = { version = \"0.5\", default-features = false }\ncipher = { version = \"0.4\", default-features = false }\ndigest = { version = \"0.10\", default-features = false }\n\n# Concrete cipher implementations used by header protection\n# (QUIC packet-number encryption is QUIC logic, not TLS).\naes = { version = \"0.8\", default-features = false }\nchacha20 = { version = \"0.9\", default-features = false }\n\n# UDP socket abstraction.  std-only.\nsocket2 = { version = \"0.5\", default-features = false, features = [\"all\"] }\n\n# Optional backend implementations (gated by `sys-*` features).\nopenssl = { version = \"0.10\", optional = true }\n","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01VP9BMAeiuLSwRrfPcQDcMZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"No `libc` crate. Let me use hardcoded values for `addr_length`:\n- `sockaddr_in` on Linux and macOS is 16 bytes\n- `sockaddr_in6` on Linux is 28 bytes, on macOS it's 28 bytes too\n\nActually, let me just hardcode these values as they're the same across Linux and macOS:\n- IPv4: 16 bytes\n- IPv6: 28 bytes\n\nNow let me also check the `socket2` crate for any useful types.\n\n\nThe `socket2` crate provides `SockAddr` which wraps a `sockaddr_storage`, so I can leverage that. Since the address sizes are consistent across our 64-bit POSIX targets, I'll hardcode them and now write out the full `utils.rs` implementation, planning carefully around the `hash_addr_bytes` and `get_ip_addr` functions.\n\nFor `hash_addr_bytes`, I need to hash the IP bytes plus port bytes—that's 6 bytes for IPv4 and 18 bytes for IPv6. The port bytes come from converting the port to native-endian bytes, matching what the C code does. For `get_ip_addr`, I'm running into a limitation where `IpAddr::octets()` returns by value rather than a reference, so I can't return a borrowed slice from the address's internal storage.\n\nI'm going to return `None` for now and document the limitation, since the callers likely handle this case or call `octets()` directly. Now I'm setting up the imports and implementing `hash_addr` to use siphash on the serialized address bytes.\n\nImplementing hash_addr... Setting up thread-local storage for debug state with RefCell to handle the mutable stream and suspension flag, then I'll use Box::leak to store the solution directory as static data.\n\nFor the solution directory, I'm using a static Mutex to hold an optional reference, and since string literals are already `'static`, test calls like `set_solution_dir(Some(\"./\"))` work without needing to copy or leak anything—though repeated calls would leak old strings, which is acceptable for test usage.\n\nBut the function signature doesn't enforce the `'static` requirement, so I can't rely on that assumption. I'll use `Box::leak` to handle any string passed in, accepting the minor memory cost for simplicity. Let me check how the tests actually use this function to see what approach makes the most sense.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3133,"cache_read_input_tokens":110942,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3133},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"3d9ae8b3-5713-4d5c-bed4-9db3f2654863"}
