ting encoding functions... Writing encoding functions... I'm writing the encoding function that first encodes the length as a variable-length integer, then copies the data into the remaining buffer space, making sure there's enough room for both the encoded length and the actual data.\n\nNow I'm comparing this to the C implementation to make sure the logic matches — specifically, the C version returns NULL if there isn't enough space even after the varint encoding succeeds, which translates to checking that the remaining slice has at least `l` bytes available.\n\nThe tricky part is handling the lifetime correctly since `frames_varlen_encode` returns a mutable subslice of the original buffer, so I need to make sure the borrow checker is satisfied when I use that returned slice.\n\nDefining the function signature... Writing encoding functions... I see the C function only returns 0 or -1, never positive values, so I'll map those to `Ordering::Equal` and `Ordering::Less` respectively. Now for the `uint8_to_str` function, I'm working through the logic for converting byte data to a string representation, handling the case where the output buffer might be too small.\n\nContinuing with the conversion logic... Looking at the Rust version more carefully, it returns a slice of the populated content without the NUL terminator, while the C function returns a pointer to the beginning of the buffer as a NUL-terminated string. The Rust approach still writes the NUL for safety but excludes it from the returned slice, which aligns with Rust's string semantics.\n\nNow I'm checking the boundary conditions—if `rendered` reaches `text.len()`, writing to `text[rendered]` would panic. The C code guards against this by ensuring `rendered + 1 < text_len` before adding dots, and the initial `render_length` calculation stops at `text_len - 4` to reserve space. But I need to verify this logic holds for edge cases where `text_len` is very small.\n\nWriting the Rust implementation... I'm working through how to handle the overflow case when checking if the data fits in the text buffer—using saturating arithmetic or a simpler comparison like `data_len >= text_len` to avoid undefined behavior in Rust while keeping the logic equivalent to the C version. I've confirmed that `data_len >= text_len` is the safer way to express this condition without risking overflow. Now I'm planning out how to organize my edits—I could do one large replacement covering multiple functions, but I think targeted replacements for each function will be safer and easier to track.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1924,"cache_read_input_tokens":66286,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1924},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c6df50c8-84e7-456d-b33a-aec683aa6926","uuid":"556ac5d4-dd59-4a89-8b9b-b1315ee7cb86"}
